\par\par \b \uc2 Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  at IDR 'B+'; Downgrades VR to 'b'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2020\par 19:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-12 October 2020: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  has affirmed \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICBC Turkey Bank A.S \f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ICBC Turkey\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) Long-Term Foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+', while downgrading the Viability Rating (VR) to 'b' from 'b+'. The Outlook on the LTFC IDR is Negative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The downgrade of the VR reflects pressure on \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's core capital position amid the coronavirus outbreak. At end-1H20, \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's reported regulatory Tier 1 ratio was 8.56% (including the impact of forbearance), below the regulatory minimum requirement of 8.76%, although the bank was compliant with its minimum CET1 and total capital ratios. As a result of the downgrade, the bank's 'B+' LTFC IDR is now driven solely by institutional support.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is provided below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS AND SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR of \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  is driven by support from its 92.8% owner, \uc2 Industrial and Commercial Bank of China Limited\uc2  (\uc2 ICBC\uc2 ; A/Stable). Our view of the propensity and ability of \uc2 ICBC\uc2  to provide support reflects the bank's ownership, strategic importance, integration and role within the wider group. However, its SR is capped at '4' due to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks in the banking system.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's rating. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks have increased, given the greater risk of a stress in Turkey's external finances amid current heightened market volatility and Turkey's weakened net sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position. This drives the Negative Outlook on the bank's rating.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in LC versus obligations in FC. However, the LTLC IDR is also on Negative Outlook mirroring the sovereign Outlook, reflecting our view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its obligations in LC is not lower than the probability of a sovereign default in LC.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The downgrade of the VR reflects very weak core capitalisation at \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  , considering its Tier 1 ratio (including the uplift from regulatory forbearance) was in breach of the regulatory minimum at end-1H20. Risks to capitalisation are further heightened by high single-name concentration risk in the bank's loan book and material FC lending due to the inflation of FC risk-weighted assets (RWAs) from lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  continues to believe that ordinary capital support for \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  from its parent will continue to be forthcoming given the bank's small size, strategic importance to its parent and role in \uc2 ICBC\uc2 's 'Road and Belt Initiative'. Nonetheless, \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's Tier 1 ratio falling below the regulatory minimum reflects core capital pressures from RWA growth, primarily relating to off-balance sheet exposures, and indicates potential delays in the timeliness of required ordinary support. This is the main driver of the VR downgrade. The bank expects a new capital injection in 1Q21 which should moderately improve its core capital ratios, and should help restore compliance with minimum Tier-1 ratio requirements.\par \par \uc2 The bank's total capital ratio is strong (21.9%) and was comfortably above the 12% regulatory minimum at end-1H20, supported by a large buffer of subordinated FC funding from \uc2 ICBC\uc2 , which provides a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 A material part of \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's funding (end-1H20: equal to 44% of total funding) is also sourced from \uc2 ICBC\uc2 , indicative of a high level of ordinary funding and liquidity support. As a result, the bank has limited FC wholesale debt exposure. Its FC liquidity is supported by a material share of stable parent funding, the absence of significant maturing wholesale funding liabilities and a high level of FC deposit funding.\par \par \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  has a limited franchise in Turkey, where it operates as a universal commercial bank mainly servicing commercial, corporate and, to a lesser extent, SME customers. Single-name concentration risk in the loan book is exacerbated by its small absolute size. The bank has a niche servicing large corporates and China-Turkey trade-related flows, but such business is limited relative to its overall \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Its strategy is also to expand lending to domestic financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 Risks to the bank's standalone credit profile were already significant coming into the pandemic and have been further heightened by the coronavirus outbreak and lockdown measures. The lira has depreciated 24% in 9M20 and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts Turkey's GDP will contract 3.2% in 2020 followed by a sharp recovery (5%) in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy measures and fiscal support for the private sector and financial markets, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  interest-rate cuts and the expanded Credit Guarantee Fund (CGF; whereby financing disbursed under the facility to SMEs and certain retail borrowers is covered by a Turkish Treasury guarantee up to a certain non-performing financing (NPF) cap), have supported borrowers' repayment capacity. However, take-up of CGF loans at \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  has been limited given its more corporate focus. Regulatory forbearance measures will provide uplift to its reported asset quality, capital and performance metrics over the short term.\par \par \uc2 At end-1H20, \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's non-performing loan I (NPL) ratio was a low 0.5% and its Stage 2 loans ratio a moderate 6.5% (the latter 43% restructured). \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects the bank to continue to outperform peers in terms of NPL origination rate. Its reported asset-quality metrics will continue to be supported by forbearance in the short term. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  does not expect sector NPLs to pick up until at least 1Q21.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks for the bank are significant given the weaker GDP growth outlook, exceptionally high FC lending (end-1H20: 82% of loans versus 35% for the sector) - given the potential impact of the lira depreciation on often weakly hedged borrowers' ability to service their debt - and significant concentration risk. Its 24 largest loans comprised 76% of gross loans at end-1H20 (or 4.6x equity). Exposure to the troubled energy sector amounted to a combined 25% of gross loans at end-1H20, a sector that has come under pressure from the weak growth outlook, lira depreciation and weak energy prices.\par \par \uc2 Mitigating the credit risk, however, is the bank's focus on generally higher-quality corporate borrowers, Turkish financial institutions and projects under government guarantee (including revenue and debt assumption guarantees and feed-in tariffs on energy projects), among others. The largely long-term, slowly amortising structure of the loan book also means credit losses are likely to feed through only gradually. Our base case is that asset-quality risks for the bank should be manageable considering \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's loan book composition and the high share of Turkish government securities and loans extended to Turkish banks in the form of bilateral and syndication loans on the bank's balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 In addition, total NPL and Stage 2 reserves coverage is strong (end-1H20: 491% and 24%, respectively), albeit sensitive to single-name risk.\par \par \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  generally reports below-sector-average profitability metrics, reflecting a lack of economies of scale, limited pricing power and a below-sector average net interest margin, due to its corporate, largely FC-denominated loan book. The bank's operating profit-to-RWAs was 1.5% in 1H20 (sector: 2.2%), but included large trading gains from the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  position. Its performance is likely to remain muted given capital constraints on growth and likely further moderate impairment charges in the weaker economic climate.\par \par \uc2 At end-1H20, \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  reported a Tier 1 ratio of 8.56% (including 149bp uplift from regulatory forbearance), below its 8.76% regulatory minimum, the latter including a 2.5% capital conservation buffer and 0.26% countercyclical buffer. Tier 1 capital is tight for its concentration risk, weak internal capital generation and the lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWA assets). Its CET1 ratio was also 8.56% (including the impact of regulatory forbearance), above the minimum regulatory requirement of 7.26%, and its reported total capital adequacy ratio of 21.9% (including forbearance) comfortably above the minimum of 12%.\par \par \uc2 Deposit funding, primarily in FC, accounted for a moderate 56% of \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2  's total funding at end-1H20. The remainder of its funding comprised parent funding, indicating high reliance on group funding.\par \par \uc2 The bank's FC liquidity position is generally moderate with additional comfort provided by the presence of funding from its parent. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  calculates that at end-1H20 FC liquid assets (comprising mainly cash and interbank placements, FC reserves held under the reserve option mechanism and unpledged government securities) sufficiently covered short-term FC non-deposit liabilities due within a year. Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure in the event of deposit instability, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Rating reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to that of other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING AND VR\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 12 Oct 2020 13:41 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 ICBC Turkey\uc2   at IDR -2-\par \par \uc2 The bank's LTFC IDR is primarily sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector and could be downgraded if we assess this risk as having increased. The LTFC IDR could also be downgraded if our assessment of institutional support simultaneously weakens.\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDR is sensitive to the ability and propensity of \uc2 ICBC\uc2  to provide support and to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 The VR is sensitive to marked deterioration in the operating environment and therefore to the economic and financial market fallout of the pandemic, given the negative implications for the bank's financial profile.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to further weakening in core capital metrics or a weakening in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the likelihood or timeliness of ordinary capital support from \uc2 ICBC\uc2  - including if the bank remains below its minimum regulatory capital requirements over a sustained period as a result of capital support being insufficient or not forthcoming on a timely basis.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade could also result from material deterioration of asset quality or a weakening of the bank's FC liquidity position due to deposit outflows, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's ratings is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTLC IDR and also in its relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ICBC Turkey Bank A.S \uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating