\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms Burgan Bank Turkey at 'B+'; Downgrades Viability Rating to 'b-'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2020\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-12 October 2020: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Burgan Bank A.S.'s (Burgan Bank Turkey) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+'. The Outlook is Negative. At the same time, Fitch has downgraded the Viability Rating (VR) to 'b-' from 'b'. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade of the VR reflects heightened pressures on the bank's performance and asset quality resulting from the coronavirus pandemic. Its core capitalisation is weak, while risks to capital from unreserved net impaired loans and potential further lira depreciation remain significant.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRs AND SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey's 'B+' LTFC IDR is driven by institutional support from its 99.6% shareholder, Kuwait-based Burgan Bank K.P.S.C. (Burgan Bank Kuwait; A+/Stable), in case of need. Our view of support reflects the bank's ownership, strategic importance to and integration with the group, and common branding. However, its Support Rating is capped at '4' due to Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks in the banking system. Burgan Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Burgan Bank Kuwait's largest international subsidiary, and represented 15% of the parent's consolidated assets at end-1H20.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's rating. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations more likely than a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 In Fitch's view, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks have increased, given the greater risk of a stress in Turkey's external finances amid current heightened market volatility and Turkey's weakened net sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position. This drives the Negative Outlook on the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Fitch has also affirmed the Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, at 'BB-', one notch above Burgan Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's LTFC IDR. This reflects our view that government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less likely than in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Outlook on the LTLC IDR is Negative, mirroring the sovereign Outlook, and reflecting our view that government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not less likely than a sovereign default in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The VR reflects the concentration of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Turkish operating environment and significant risks to its credit profile from the economic and financial-market fallout from the coronavirus. These factors have heightened pressures on asset quality, performance and capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey's core capitalisation (1H20: common equity Tier 1 - CET1 - ratio of 10%, with forbearance) is weak given its risk profile, fairly high unreserved impaired loans (1H20: equal to 22% of CET1 capital), asset-quality pressures, concentration risks and the lira depreciation (given the inflation of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk-weighted assets). Its loss-absorption buffer has been eroded, while its internal capital generation has also declined significantly.\par \par \uc2 Risks to Burgan Bank Turkey's standalone creditworthiness were already significant coming into the pandemic and have been further heightened by the coronavirus outbreak and lockdown measures, given the economic contraction and market volatility. The lira depreciated by 24% in 9M20 and Fitch forecasts Turkey's real GDP will contract 3.2% in 2020, followed by a subsequent sharp recovery (5.0% growth) in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy measures and fiscal support for the private sector and financial markets, including further lira interest-rate cuts in 1H20 and the expanded Credit Guarantee Fund (whereby financing disbursed under the facility largely to SMEs is covered by a Turkish Treasury guarantee up to a certain percentage of non-performing loans), have supported borrower repayment capacity and sector loan growth over the course of the pandemic. However, Credit Guarantee Fund-backed loans comprised a small 3% of Burgan Bank Turkey's gross loans at end-1H20.\par \par \uc2 Regulatory forbearance in place until end-2020 will continue to provide uplift to the bank's reported asset quality, capital and performance metrics over the short term. Asset quality-related forbearance measures may serve only to delay recognition of problematic exposures, in our view.\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey has a limited franchise in Turkey. The bank's very small size (ranked among the top 30 banks in Turkey) and market shares (end-1H20: 0.4% of sector assets) result in limited competitive advantages and pricing power.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks for the bank are significant given material foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending (end-1H20: 67% of gross loans) and the potential impact of lira depreciation on often weakly hedged borrowers' ability to service their debt, high single-name risk (the 25 largest cash exposures were equal to 54% of gross loans or an exceptionally high 5.3x CET1 at end-1H20) and exposure to risky sectors. Loan growth was 12.6% in 1H20 (2% on an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted basis, compared with 16% for the sector).\par \par \uc2 Lumpy project finance lending, largely to the commercial real estate and renewable energy sectors, comprised 25% of gross loans at end-1H20. The largely long-term, slowly amortising structure of the loan book creates seasoning risks and means credit losses are likely to feed through only gradually. The bank also has concentration risk to vulnerable sectors, including construction and real estate (end-1H20: 19% of gross loans), which has come under pressure from the weak growth outlook and lira depreciation, and tourism (14%), which has been negatively affected by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The bank's weakening asset quality is a key rating driver of the VR. The bank's impaired loans (NPLs) ratio has risen sharply since 2016 and increased to a fairly high 8.6% at end-1H20, significantly above the sector average of 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 We believe the bank's NPL ratio will exceed 10% by end-2020 due to the migration of loans overdue by 90-180 days (equal to a high 5.7% of gross loans at end-1H20) classified as Stage 2 due to regulatory forbearance. Stage 2 loans were a high 20% of gross loans (over half were restructured) at end-1H20.\par \par \uc2 Total reserves coverage of impaired loans is weak (end-1H20: 74%; sector: 116%), partly reflecting reliance on collateral (including for leases), which is likely to be harder to realise given operating environment pressures. Specific reserves coverage of Stage 2 loans (end-1H20: 18%) was above that of most peers. However, single-name risk could result in volatility in reserve coverage levels.\par \par \uc2 The bank's operating profit/risk-weighted assets ratio was negligible in 1H20 due to high loan impairment charges that consumed 98% of pre-impairment operating profit and a decrease in the net interest margin (3.8%; 2019: 4.4%) partly given higher cash balances with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pre-impairment operating profitability is below the sector average, reflecting a lack of economies of scale and diversification, limited pricing power and concentration on corporate lending where margins are relatively low. Profitability is likely to remain weak, due to high impairments and low growth in the weaker economic climate.\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey's CET1 ratio was broadly flat at 10% in 1H20 but includes +90bp uplift from regulatory forbearance. Core capitalisation is weak in light of its risk profile, pressured asset quality (end-1H20: unreserved impaired loans were equal to 22% of CET1 capital; end-2019: 15%) and weak earnings generation (1H20: return on average equity of 0.3%).\par \par \uc2 Core capital is also sensitive to lira depreciation given the bank's high share of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans. However, pre-impairment operating profit (1H20: 2.5% of annualised average loans) provides a limited buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey's total capital adequacy ratio is also higher (19.4% at end-1H20, broadly in line with the sector average) and comfortably above the regulatory minimum. It comprises a large buffer of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subordinated debt (equivalent to 10.5% of risk-weighted assets at end-1H20) from Burgan Bank Kuwait that provides a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Funding is primarily sourced from customer deposits (end-1H20: 56% of total funding), but a significant and increased share is in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (70% of total customer deposits; sector: 50%). Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale funding is also significant (42% of total funding at end-1H20), which creates refinancing risks for the bank amid increased operating environment pressures and market volatility.\par \par \uc2 However, about three-quarters of external debt is provided by the bank's parent via foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bilateral borrowings and long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subordinated debt. The VR consequently also reflects heavy reliance on group funding.\par \par \uc2 The bank's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity position is adequate, with additional comfort provided by the presence of its parent. Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity is broadly sufficient to cover total foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale funding falling due within 12 months but could come under pressure in case of prolonged market closure or deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating has been affirmed at 'AA(tur)' with a Stable Outlook, reflecting our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 IDRs, SUPPORT RATING, VR\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's IDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if Fitch believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated.\par \par \uc2 Fitch could upgrade the VR if the bank demonstrates sustainable improvement in asset quality and profitability, which reduces risks to core capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LTFC IDR is primarily sensitive to Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector and could be downgraded if we assess this risk as having increased. The LTFC IDR could also be downgraded if our assessment of institutional support simultaneously weakens.\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDR is sensitive to the ability and propensity of its parent to provide support and to Fitch's view of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 The VR is sensitive to a marked deterioration in the operating environment and therefore to the economic and financial market fallout of the pandemic, given the negative implications for the bank's financial profile.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded as a result of a further material deterioration in asset quality or profitability beyond our expectations or a sharp increase in unreserved impaired loans if this further erodes the bank's core capital position and weakens its loss-absorption capacity.\par \par \uc2 A VR downgrade could also result from a weakening in the bank's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity position due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTLC IDR and also in its relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Burgan Bank Turkey are linked to the ratings of Burgan Bank Kuwait.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on Fitch's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2