\par\par \b \uc2 Credit Markets: Foreign Buyers Flee Turkey's Bond Market as Economy Reels -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Caitlin Ostroff \par \uc2 1,194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (July 25, 2020).\par \par \uc2 Bond investors are retreating from Turkey, adding to the pressure on an economy long seen as dependent on foreign funding.\par \par \uc2 Fund managers withdrew more than $7 billion from Turkey's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond market in the six months ended in June, making it the largest drawdown in the first half on record, according to data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nonresidents' ownership of outstanding Turkish government bonds has melted to about 5%, from nearly a third in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The bonds have grown less attractive to investors as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed interest rates to less than the inflation rate this year, sending the real yield on the debt below zero.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey is becoming irrelevant for fixed-income investors," said Viktor Szab\u243\'00\'F3, investment director at Aberdeen Standard Investments. "The way they pulled out of Turkey is really unprecedented."\par \par \uc2 The money flowing out of Turkey stands to add to the woes of the heavily indebted country, which has been struggling for years with a dependency on overseas capital. This year, coronavirus lockdowns have eroded the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's purchases of cars and textiles from Turkey, and wiped out most of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenue from tourism.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Szab\u243\'00\'F3 has almost completely exited its local debt market since the start of the year. He began paring back holdings when \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to limit the lira's depreciation in March.\par \par \uc2 To halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  burned through billions of dollars of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and borrowed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from domestic banks, selling them to buy lira. Despite those efforts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May slumped to a record low against the U.S. dollar before edging up, leaving its retreat this year at about 13%.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the lira fell to the all-time low in early May, Turkey blocked three international banks from trading its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , accusing them of failing to fulfill their liabilities in transactions with Turkish banks. The move was widely seen as an effort to block investors from betting against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ban was lifted within days.\par \par \uc2 With the lira sliding this year, Turkey's overseas borrowing costs have risen despite the country benefiting from cheaper oil and gas. That is making foreign investors leery.\par \par \uc2 The yield on a five-year U.S. dollar bond that comes due in March 2025 has climbed to 5.66% Thursday, from about 4.71% when Turkey issued it in February, according to FactSet. The yield on its 30-year dollar bond climbed to 7.08% on Thursday, from less than 5% four years ago. Both are rated below investment grade by \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Nobody wants to buy long-dated bonds in Turkey anymore. It's one of the signs of stress," said \uc2 Jan Dehn\uc2 , head of research at \uc2 Ashmore Group\uc2 , who has sold Turkish debt holdings in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pressure on the economy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , President Recep Tayyip Erdo an last year began trying to bolster economic growth by driving down borrowing costs. At his urging, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed interest rates to 8.25%, from 24% in 2019. At its meeting Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  left rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 "It's stop-go economics with these guys: They love growth, they love credit, they want to create new jobs, but it's never balanced," said Timothy Ash, senior sovereign strategist covering emerging markets at \uc2 BlueBay Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the first half of this year, Turkish banks issued loans to households at a record pace. The government is also guaranteeing debt to small and medium-size businesses to keep credit flowing. Investors are increasingly worried about how borrowers will repay that debt.\par \par \uc2 Such concerns prompted foreign investors to also pull $4 billion from Turkish stocks this year, bringing foreign ownership under 50% for the first time in 16 years, according to Matriks Data. Earlier this month, Turkey temporarily banned six foreign banks from short selling stocks, which allows investors to bet on a share price declining. The MSCI has threatened to reclassify Turkey as a frontier market, saying that accessibility to the stock market has been impacted by such bans.\par \par \uc2 The explosion of cheap credit in Turkey has resulted in increased consumption and demand for imported goods. Through May, imports exceeded exports by $16 billion, putting the current-account deficit on track to reach $40 billion, or its widest since 2017, by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Normally, countries would issue long-term debt to fund that gap. Turkey would likely struggle to attract foreign investors because of its grim economic picture.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now owes more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it has in its coffers, analysts say, and it is using that borrowed money to finance the gap between imports and exports, they add.\par \par \uc2 An official with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the drop in the institution's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves should be considered "as normal" in the current global crisis environment. "Reserves are considered to be sufficient in terms of external financing needs and it is considered that there will be no financing problems in the coming period," the official said in an email.\par \par \uc2 Turkey can continue to borrow foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its banks so long as its domestic banks are able to convince foreign banks to lend to them, and residents who hold foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits in Turkey's banks keep them there, analysts said. But the country would be more vulnerable to shocks should either change.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's dependence on its reserves and borrowed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has left investors speculating that the nation is edging ever closer to a balance-of-payments crisis. That would leave the country unable to pay for essential imports or make payments on its external debt. Turkey to solve the crisis would be forced to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken and raise interest rates, reducing economic growth, or seek a bailout, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 You can keep the game going, but you add to the risk of a really bad outcome," said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --David Gauthier-Villars contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications The yield on a five-year U.S. dollar bond that comes due in March 2025 has climbed to 5.66% on Thursday, from about 4.71% when Turkey issued it in February, according to FactSet. The yield on its 30-year dollar bond climbed to 7.08% on Thursday, from less than 5% four years ago. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said the increase on the yield on a five-year U.S. dollar bond was for this week, and the yield increase on the 30-year bond was for Tuesday. (Corrected on July 24, 2020)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 25, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200725eg7p0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
