\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Turkey Ratings; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 4,370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2020\par 22:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 24 Jul 2020 16:18 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Turkey Ratings; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We expect the Turkish economy to contract by 3.3% in real terms this \par year, due to the impact of the pandemic. \par      -- Although we expect economic growth to recover in the second half of \par the year, there are signs of past imbalances re-emerging, including the rapid \par pace of credit growth, double-digit inflation, and a widening of the current \par account deficit in recent months. \par      -- Nevertheless, unless Turkish banks require large-scale public support, \par we forecast that by end-2020, net general government debt will amount to a \par contained 35% of GDP, leaving fiscal room to maneuver. \par      -- Consequently, we are affirming our unsolicited 'B+/B' foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par and 'BB-/B' local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Turkey. The outlook is \par stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On July 24, 2020, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its unsolicited long-term \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating on Turkey at 'B+' and its unsolicited \par long-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating at 'BB-'. The outlook is \par stable. \par  \par We affirmed the unsolicited short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign \par credit ratings at 'B'. \par  \par We also affirmed the unsolicited national scale ratings at trAA+/--/trA-1+. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook balances the lingering downside economic risks stemming \par from the pandemic over the next 12 months against the resilience of Turkey's \par private sector, and the still-contained stock of net general government debt. \par We expect that, despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility and COVID-19-related interruptions \par to economic activity, Turkish GDP will recover in the second half of this \par year. \par  \par Downside scenario \par We could lower the ratings if we saw a sustained re-emergence of macroeconomic \par imbalances, such as a persistently high pace of credit growth and the current \par account deficits widening by more than our forecasts. Such imbalances, in \par turn, would increase the likelihood of banking system distress, which implies \par contingent liability risks for public finances. We could also lower the \par ratings if Turkey's still comparatively strong public finances deteriorated \par for other reasons, such as the budgetary impact of COVID-19 being much larger \par than expected or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility inflating general government debt levels \par beyond our projections. \par  \par Upside scenario \par We could consider an upgrade if Turkey's growth proved to be higher and more \par sustainable than we forecast over the medium term while external imbalances \par declined and net general government debt remained contained. We could also \par raise the ratings if the government successfully devised and implemented a \par credible and transparent economic reform program focused on structural \par macroeconomic improvements, including strengthening the banking system as well \par as bolstering the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) reserves and monetary policy \par effectiveness of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Republic of Turkey\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 ). \par  \par RATIONALE \par We consider that, as in other countries, the pandemic will have a negative \par effect on Turkey's economy. Although the virus now appears under control, with \par a steady decline in the number of new registered cases, we expect economic \par normalization to be a gradual process. In our view, both domestic demand and \par exports are affected and the tourism sector has been hit particularly hard, \par given disruptions to cross-border movements and more cautious attitudes to \par travel. On a positive note, Turkey and Russia earlier came to an agreement to \par resume flights and we anticipate that travel arrangements with several other \par European countries should also gradually normalize. Overall, we forecast that \par the Turkish economy will contract by 3.3% in real terms in 2020. \par  \par The authorities have adopted a number of fiscal measures to help the economy \par deal with the pandemic fallout. These include additional health care \par expenditure, tax deferrals, and contributions to salary payments for affected \par employees and businesses. A substantial part of the policy response also \par appears in the form of a sizable credit impulse, with a rapid increase in the \par amount of new credit issued by domestic banks. \par  \par This credit impulse is delivered directly, via state-owned banks, as well as \par through a series of measures that incentivize private banks to lend. Although \par supportive of near-term growth, there are signs that an outsized credit \par stimulus could underpin re-emergence of economic imbalances that frequently \par characterized Turkish economy in the past. Specifically: \par      -- The stock of domestic credit has already expanded by almost 24% since \par the start of the year; \par      -- Following a decline throughout 2019, year-on-year inflation has \par plateaued at around 11%-13%, and the consumer price index (CPI) rose by 12.6% \par in June, year on year; \par      -- This year's cumulative current account deficit has exceeded 2% of GDP \par through end-May, compared with a small surplus for the same period last year. \par  \par Our baseline expectation is that this credit expansion will gradually moderate \par in the coming months and the economy will recover in a sustainable manner and \par without further imbalances building up. However, if unchecked, continued \par credit stimulus could ultimately make Turkey more vulnerable to a disruptive \par economic adjustment, similar to the one it experienced in August 2018. This is \par particularly relevant now, against the background of heightened global \par economic uncertainty and more volatile fund flows to emerging markets. \par  \par Positively, we project that Turkey's net general government debt will remain \par modest as a percentage of GDP, even after factoring in the negative budgetary \par impact of the pandemic. We forecast that net general government debt will \par total 35% of GDP at the end of 2020, providing some fiscal headroom in the \par event of a further hypothetical negative shock. \par  \par Our ratings on Turkey remain constrained by what we view as its weak \par institutions. We see limited checks and balances between government bodies, \par with power concentrated in the hands of the executive branch, which renders \par policy responses difficult to predict. Nevertheless, the outcome of local \par elections held in 2019 suggests that Turkey's political system retains a \par degree of competition. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: Recession in 2020 owing to the impact of \par COVID-19 pandemic \par      -- We forecast the Turkish economy will contract by 3.3% this year. \par      -- The pandemic appears under control, which should allow for a quicker \par recovery compared with other emerging market economies. \par      -- Policy and institutional risks remain elevated. \par  \par The COVID-19 pandemic had a negative effect on the Turkish economy; directly, \par by eroding domestic demand, and indirectly, by constraining foreign trade and \par tourism. \par  \par We forecast that world GDP will contract by 3.8% this year (see "The Global \par Economy Begins A Slow Mend As COVID-19 Eases Unevenly," published on July 1, \par 2020). Moreover, we project a 7.8% output contraction in 2020 in the eurozone, \par the market for over 30% of Turkish goods exports. The Turkish tourism sector \par is also being particularly hard hit as international borders reopen and air \par travel resumes only gradually. As such, we now expect that this summer's \par tourism season is likely to be largely lost. Consequently, Turkey's exports \par will contract by 12% in real terms or around 20% in U.S. dollar terms this \par year. \par  \par We forecast both Turkey's domestic consumption and investments will contract, \par reflecting high uncertainty; diminished consumer purchasing power; and a \par weaker Turkish lira, which will increase the burden of servicing \par foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated debt and thereby limit the scope for investments. \par  \par Positively, Turkey now appears to have controlled the rate of \par infection--although the total number of COVID-19 cases registered to date has \par surpassed 200,000, currently active cases are much lower, at close to 13,000. \par This contrasts with other emerging market economies, such as Russia, Brazil, \par or South Africa, where cases are still rising at a rapid pace. The \par more-positive dynamic of the pandemic, in turn, should allow the authorities \par to ease the social distancing and movement restrictions further and underpin a \par quicker recovery, compared with other emerging markets. Turkey has been easing \par social distancing measures since May. \par  \par To cushion the impact of the pandemic, the authorities have adopted a number \par of fiscal and credit stimulus measures. The latter have already underpinned \par credit growth in excess of 20% over the first six months of the year. Although \par supportive of short-term economic growth, we consider that, if left unchecked, \par the large credit impulse could pose medium-term risks for the Turkish economy. \par  \par Overall, our baseline scenario has the Turkish economy contracting by 3.3% in \par real terms in 2020 before recovering by 4.5% in 2021. Our medium-term growth \par forecasts are unchanged, with growth at 3.5% on average in 2022 and 2023. \par  \par We consider that Turkey's institutional arrangements remain comparatively weak \par and continue to constrain the sovereign ratings. In the June 2018 presidential \par and parliamentary elections, the president and the Adalet ve Kalkinma Partisi \par (AKP)-led coalition secured a victory that was the final chapter in Turkey's \par transition to an executive presidential system. We see limited checks and \par balances between government bodies. Because power is concentrated in the hands \par of the executive branch, it is difficult to predict policy responses. \par  \par Nevertheless, a degree of domestic political competition remains, as \par highlighted by the results of last year's local elections, when opposition \par parties secured the mayorships of several large cities, including Istanbul, \par Ankara, and Izmir, and the electoral authorities acknowledged these results. \par  \par We also consider that some geopolitical risks remain, including from Turkish \par military involvement in Northern Syria. There are also risks from possible \par international sanctions from the U.S., related to Turkey's earlier purchase of \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Jul 2020 16:18 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Turkey Ratings; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia's S-400 anti-aircraft missiles; and the EU , related to Turkey's gas \par exploration and drilling activities around Cyprus. \par  \par Flexibility and performance profile: Signs of imbalances re-emerging, but \par fiscal headroom remains \par      -- Turkey is experiencing rapid domestic credit growth against the \par background of elevated inflation and a widening current account deficit. \par      -- CBRT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves have declined and deteriorated in quality since the \par start of the year. \par      -- Fiscal space remains, given comparatively low net general government \par debt of 35% of GDP, even after the impact of the pandemic is taken into \par account. \par  \par Throughout 2020, the Turkish authorities have swiftly introduced a number of \par fiscal and monetary measures to shield the domestic economy from the impact of \par the pandemic. While the direct budgetary measures are fairly muted (estimated \par at a total of around 2% of GDP), the focus has been on a large credit \par stimulus. The authorities have introduced several initiatives: \par      -- Loan payment moratoriums offered by public banks to affected firms, \par initially for three months; \par      -- Bank regulator BRSA implemented the new asset ratio regulation, which \par incentivizes commercial banks to lend; \par      -- The recognition period for Stage 2 loans and nonperforming loans has \par been extended; and \par      -- A few other forbearance measures were introduced, including on the \par liquidity coverage ratio and the calculation of capital adequacy. \par  \par As a result, domestic credit has been expanding at a very high pace recently, \par even compared with similar episodes of monetary impulse in Turkey in the past. \par Over just the first six months of 2020, the stock of nonfinancial sector loans \par grew by almost 24%. For comparison, this exceeds full-year credit growth in \par Turkey for every year between 2014 and 2019. We understand that this new bank \par credit is partially replacing foreign debt as the nonbank corporate sector has \par been reducing external leverage. Nevertheless, even adjusting for this effect, \par the rate of credit expansion is rapid. We have revised our forecast upward and \par now expect loan growth to reach 30% this year. \par  \par Although the recent credit impulse could support a speedier economic recovery \par over the short term, we consider that it could ultimately reignite some of the \par imbalances that have frequently emerged within the Turkish economy in the \par past. For example, a large credit stimulus through 2017 and the first half of \par 2018 ended abruptly with the August 2018 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis, which precipitated \par an output contraction, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility, and a spike in inflation. \par  \par In recent months, inflation appears to have plateaued at 11%-13%, with June \par CPI expanding by 12.6% year on year. Meanwhile, the current account deficit \par has been widening--it totaled a cumulative US$17 billion over January-May \par 2020, compared with a small surplus for the same period in 2019. In our view, \par this adds to Turkey's existing balance of payments vulnerabilities, which stem \par from the large stock of external debt it accumulated in the past, \par predominantly within the country's banking sector. Positively, the banks have \par been able to roll over their maturing foreign debt throughout 2020, with \par rollover rates of 80%-90%. We expect them to continue to roll over debt \par unhindered, but risks remain elevated, particularly if fund flows to emerging \par markets, which have returned following massive outflows in March 2020, were to \par reverse again. \par  \par In parallel, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves have trended down and deteriorated in \par quality as its borrowing has picked up via swap lines on the liability side in \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . We net these obligations out and expect usable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par to drop below US$10 billion this year, from around US$30 billion last year, \par before staging a gradual recovery over the medium term. In our view, this \par limits the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 's firepower to counteract further exchange rate volatility or \par to meet unexpected external financing requirements. \par  \par In our view, Turkey's fiscal position remains comparatively strong and \par continues to support the sovereign ratings. We project that, as a result of \par the implemented support measures and a 3.3% contraction in real output this \par year, the general government deficit will widen to 5% of GDP in 2020, from \par 3.2% in 2019. However, the increase in public leverage will be higher, \par primarily because of the depreciation of the Turkish lira. Close to half of \par government debt is denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In addition, back in \par May, the government made a capital injection into state banks worth 0.5% of \par GDP, via the Sovereign Wealth Fund. We expect net general government debt to \par total 35% of GDP at the end of 2020, which leaves the government fiscal policy \par space to maneuver. \par  \par Although fiscal leverage remains low, we consider that there are risks from \par contingent liabilities. We estimate that direct government guarantees and \par commitments under public-private partnership agreements remain limited, at \par below 10% of GDP. That said, in an adverse scenario, we anticipate that the \par government may have to extend support to the financial sector, particularly \par the public banks. We note that two cases of recapitalization have already \par happened over the last 18 months (0.7% of GDP in April 2019 and 0.5% of GDP in \par May 2020) and more support may be required, because of the rapid loan growth \par combined with the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on Turkish households and \par the corporate sector. \par  \par Bank asset quality is likely to deteriorate in the coming months. The Turkish \par lira is currently trading at about 6.9 lira to the U.S. dollar, having \par depreciated by close to 14% since the start of the year. The weaker exchange \par rate puts additional pressure on the domestic corporate sector, given that a \par significant proportion of its debt is still denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par despite the reduction in its short open \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  position. \par  \par We see Turkey's monetary policy as historically ineffective at managing \par inflation. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  has never met the 5% medium-term target introduced in \par 2012, while Turkey's real effective exchange rate (REER) has shown substantial \par swings. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  has faced increasing political pressure in recent years, \par which we consider impairs its effectiveness, often by delaying timely \par responses to rising inflation. Inflation soared to 25% in October 2018, but \par has since moderated. It currently stands at close to 12% (according to \par official data) in year-on-year terms. \par  \par Political pressure on the independence of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues. The president \par dismissed the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  governor in July 2019, just weeks ahead of a key decision \par on interest rates. Subsequently, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  quickly lowered the key repo rate by \par a cumulative 1,575 basis points over a year. In real terms, we consider policy \par rates negative, which present risks to balance-of-payment flows and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par market. \par  \par We classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Turkey\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as a managed float, with intermittent \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Although outright \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales to directly defend \par the exchange rate have generally been limited, there have been reports of \par public banks selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the lira at difficult junctures. The \par regulators have also introduced limits on domestic banks' allowed derivative \par positions with nonresidents. The aim is to curb potential speculation against \par the exchange rate. \par  \par The long-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating on Turkey is one notch higher than the \par long-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating. In our view, the managed float exchange \par rate regime, comparatively developed local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capital markets, and the \par fact that about 50% of government debt is denominated in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par almost entirely held domestically imply a lower default risk on Turkey's \par lira-denominated sovereign commercial debt than on its foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated debt. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, July 14, 2020. An interactive version is \par also available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, July 8, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, July 8, 2020 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2019 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, May 18, 2020 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2020, April 8, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Debt 2020: Emerging Market EMEA Borrowing Will Likely Inch \par Up 0.6% To $473.3 Billion, Feb. 20, 2020 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment Update (updated monthly) \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and