\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish state banks short  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  to support lira, say sources and data\b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2020\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 16 (Reuters) - Turkish state banks have doubled their short foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in six weeks to $8.3 billion to help defend the lira, their largest direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years, according to four banking sources and industry data.\par \par \uc2 The combined short positions of the state banks, shown in data from financial sector regulator BDDK, adds to more than $90 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last year by bankers' estimates for a total of about $100 billion used to support the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lira has faced downward pressure from falling interest rates over the last year to support economic growth - vital to President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's long political dominance - and limit fallout from the coronavirus crisis.\par \par \uc2 State lenders have frequently sold dollars borrowed from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, barring brief interruptions, kept a net neutral \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position since Turkey's financial crisis in the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 That changed in mid-December, when they embarked on the sharpest expansion of their short foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in at least five years, reaching a net short position of $4.3 billion on May 22 and nearly doubling that by July 3, according to BDDK statistics.\par \par \uc2 That pushed them beyond the regulatory limit of 20% of capital, though some leeway is allowed to exceed briefly.\par \par \uc2 The large short positions could expose the banks to losses if the lira suddenly drops. It was unclear if the state banks had hedged their positions to minimise that risk.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, private banks are $4.1 billion long on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to BDDK figures.\par \par \uc2 The large increase in state banks' short \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  position has coincided with greater stability of the lira, which is trading in an unusually narrow range after settling in mid-May and staying almost unmoved from 6.85 to the dollar since mid-June.\par \par \uc2 "I suspect state banks are giving a helping hand as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves are pretty much all used up," one senior Turkish banking source said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was calculated to have sold around $60 billion of reserves this year on top of $32 billion last year, while the combined exposure of the deposit-taking state banks' short \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions was over 25% of their reserves, the banking source said.\par \par \uc2 Among emerging markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unusual in that its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have depleted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves.\par \par \uc2 Its gross reserves fell from $81 billion at the start of the year to $49 billion in mid-May, just as state banks - mainly \uc2 Halkbank\uc2 , \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  and \uc2 Ziraat Bank\uc2  - started increasing their short foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, according to three banking sources and BDDK.\par \par \uc2 Halkbank\uc2  declined to comment, while \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  and \uc2 Ziraat Bank\uc2  did not immediately respond to requests. Asked whether it had coordinated with the state banks as they stepped up their short positions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not comment.\par \par \uc2 Banking regulator BDDK also declined to comment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows reserves had recovered slightly to $51 billion by July 3, the day BDDK reported the biggest short positions for state banks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by state banks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decreasing reserves heighten investor concerns about Turkey's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buffers. External debt obligations for the next 12 months are nearly $165 billion.\par \par \uc2 Despite losing around 13% of its value so far this year, the lira has still outperformed most emerging market peers. Keeping the lira both a competitive export \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stable has been one of Ankara's goals for the past few years.\par \par \uc2 Though they have not commented on state banks' short \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, Finance Minister \uc2 Berat Albayrak\uc2  said in May the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and its governor \uc2 Murat Uysal\uc2  said in April reserves had fluctuated due to "extraordinary circumstances" with the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Andrew Cawthorne) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-7-16T07:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200716eg7g00rgx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
