\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Floods Into Syria -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabel Coles in Beirut and David Gauthier-Villars in Istanbul \par \uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 25, 2020).\par \par \uc2 Syrians who want to buy a loaf of bread or fill their gas tanks in parts of the country's north are rushing to exchange Syrian pounds for Turkish lira, as businesses switch \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to insulate themselves against a crumbling economy after years of war.\par \par \uc2 "I was shocked to see that they only accept the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now," said software engineer Malek Tarboush last week, after eating at a restaurant in the northwestern Syrian city of Idlib.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is rolling out its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in parts of Syria under its control as it attempts to exploit its neighbor's economic unraveling to further entrench itself in the north of a country ravaged by the multisided conflict.\par \par \uc2 "The use of the Turkish lira is yet another indication that Turkey is seeing this as an extension of its rule and is going to have a long-term presence there," said Asli Aydintasbas, a senior policy fellow at the \uc2 European Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ankara sent shipments of Turkish lira to its allies in Idlib province and other parts of northern Syria after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  collapsed last month, according to Omer Ozkizilcik, an analyst with the SETA Foundation, an Ankara-based think tank seen as close to the Erdogan government.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira are being injected through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -exchange points and post offices, said Mr. Ozkizilcik, who closely follows Syria. Residents are swapping Syrian pounds and sometimes U.S. dollars for the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. Exchange shops in northern Syria have been thronged with customers, residents said.\par \par \uc2 An official with Turkey's finance ministry said he had no information on how Ankara was facilitating a shift to the Turkish lira in parts of Syria. The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it wasn't involved in the plan.\par \par \uc2 After nine years of war, Russia, Iran and Turkey are seeking to consolidate their gains after backing different sides in a conflict that began as a popular uprising against the government of President \uc2 Bashar al-Assad\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After multiple military \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past four years, \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls a string of areas along its southern border representing roughly 5% of its neighbor's territory. There are an estimated four million Syrians in the territory under Turkey's control, according to the \uc2 United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira was already being used to pay fighters and bureaucrats in administrative bodies backed by Ankara, which has also built schools, hospitals and post offices in the areas under its control, and connected some to its own electricity grid.\par \par \uc2 Replacing the Syrian pound with the Turkish lira knits parts of northern Syria more tightly into the fabric of Turkey's economy.\par \par \uc2 Turkey says its presence in northern Syria is necessary to protect its border against groups it regards as terrorist threats and to support rebels who oppose Mr. Assad.\par \par \uc2 That strategy, however, has put Turkey on a collision course with Russia, one of Mr. Assad's main military backers.\par \par \uc2 After fierce fighting this year that pitted the Russia and Iran-backed Syrian forces against rebel fighters and thousands of Turkish ground troops, Turkish President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  and his Russian counterpart \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  agreed on enforcing a fragile cease-fire in the area in March. The clash displaced nearly 1 million people.\par \par \uc2 But both sides have been massing forces there in recent weeks, raising concerns of a renewed offensive. Turkish forces stationed in the Idlib countryside fired rockets at Syrian military positions overnight on Monday, the \uc2 Syrian Observatory for Human Rights\uc2  reported.\par \par \uc2 The Syrian government didn't respond to a request for comment. It hasn't commented on the adoption of the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but has vowed to reclaim all its territory.\par \par \uc2 For Syrians living in Turkish-controlled territories, the rollout of the Turkish lira is a means to preserve their purchasing power against the rapid erosion of the pound, which has lost around 70% of its value against the dollar since the start of the year. The Turkish lira fell to a low last month, but has only lost 16% against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "Just like when our homes were destroyed and we had to flee: We have no other choice," said Outhman Khani, an activist based in the city of Idlib.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in the areas held by Syrian rebels backed by Turkey turned to Ankara for help last month, according to Mr. Ozkizilcik, the SETA analyst.\par \par \uc2 "The aim is to replace the Syrian pound entirely, protecting the civilians in opposition-held areas from the bad economy and the possible side effects of sanctions, and to increase pressure on the Assad regime economically," Mr. Ozkizilcik said.\par \par \uc2 Abdurrahman Mustafa, the head of the Syrian Interim Government, a Turkish-backed opposition political body that has sought to act as an umbrella coalition for governance described the adoption of the lira as a temporary measure that will be reversed when a comprehensive political solution to the conflict is found.\par \par \uc2 The Assad regime has sought to blame growing hardships on U.S. sanctions, but economists say they are the result of years of mismanagement, conflict and corruption.\par \par \uc2 Many Syrians are resigned to the new reality.\par \par \uc2 "I see [the Turkish lira] as a permanent replacement," said Mr. Tarboush, the software engineer. "The Syrian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't return to what it was before."\par \par \uc2 --Nazih Osseiran in Beirut contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Isabel Coles at isabel.coles@wsj.com and David Gauthier-Villars at David.Gauthier-Villars@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 25, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200625eg6p000dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
