\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Turkey's 3 Development Banks; Revises TSKB's Outlook to Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 4,948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2020\par 20:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 22 May 2020 14:44 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turkey's 3 Development Banks; Revises TSKB's Outlook to Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-22 May 2020:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed the Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) of Turkey's three development banks. At the same time, it has revised the Outlook on the Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR (LTFC IDR) of Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S. (TSKB) to Negative from Stable.\par \par \uc2 The Outlooks on the other two development banks, Turkiye Kalkinma ve Yatirim Bankasi A.S. (TKYB) and Turkiye Ihracat Kredi Bankasi A.S. (\uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  ), remain Stable. rkiye Sinai\par \par \uc2 The Outlook revision on TSKB reflects the sovereign's weaker foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) reserves position, and therefore reduced ability to support the bank in FC in case of need. This has driven the downward revision of its Support Rating Floor (SRF) to 'B' from 'B+', as a result of which TSKB's LTFC IDR is now driven by the bank's Viability Rating (VR) of 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TSKB's VR reflects the bank's still adequate, albeit weaker, capital and FC liquidity buffers and niche policy role. At the same time, risks to its Standalone Credit Profile have increased as a result of the economic downturn and financial-market volatility, which heighten pressure on asset quality, capitalisation, performance, funding and liquidity. This drives the Negative Outlook on TSKB's LTFC IDR, and the bank's rating could be downgraded if the recovery of the Turkish economy is slower and the impact of the pandemic on the bank more severe than currently expected.\par \par \uc2 As is usual for development banks, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  does not assign VRs to TKYB or \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  . This is because their business models, in our view, are strongly dependent on support from the state. This is reflected in their dedicated policy and development roles. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  assigns TSKB a VR based on our view that TSKB's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notwithstanding the bank's important policy role, are sufficiently independent for \uc2 Fitch\uc2  to assess the bank's standalone creditworthiness.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 IDRS; SUPPORT RATINGS; SUPPORT RATING FLOORS; SENIOR DEBT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDRs of TKYB and \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  are at the level of their respective Support Rating Floors (SRFs) of 'BB-' and 'B+', and their Support Ratings are affirmed at '3' and '4', respectively. This reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of a high government propensity to support the banks in case of need, based on their state ownership, policy roles, significant Treasury-guaranteed (TKYB) and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) funding (\uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  ) and the record of support. The banks each received additional tier 1 capital injections from the Turkish authorities in April 2019 and expect a further TRY750 million in 2H20.\par \par \uc2 TKYB focuses on direct and apex lending (the latter channelled through commercial banks and leasing companies) largely in FC to Turkish SMEs and corporates in sectors (eg. renewable energy) and regions deemed strategic by the government. Its strategy is expanding to include the development of investment-banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it plans to set up both venture-capital and private-equity funds.\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  is the country's official credit export agency. It mainly provides short-term trade finance to Turkish exporters, mostly through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rediscount loan programme and export credit insurance. Its policy role is reinforced through regulatory privileges, whereby any losses incurred by \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  in its credit, insurance and guarantee transactions arising from political risks are compensated by the Turkish Treasury, and the bank is exempt from corporate taxes and reserve requirements.\par \par \uc2 TKYB's higher SRF of 'BB-', which is equalised with Turkey's sovereign rating, reflects the bank's very low FC wholesale funding net of Treasury-guaranteed funding and the medium-term tenor of the bank's non-guaranteed funding (no maturities fall due before 2023). As a result, the bank's potential need for support over the rating horizon is limited. The Stable Outlook on TKYB's LTFC IDR reflects our expectation that the bank's level of external market funding will not increase significantly in the near- term.\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's SRF of 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR, reflects risks to the ability of the sovereign to provide support in FC, given the bank's considerably larger balance sheet and volumes of external market funding than TKYB's and in light of the sovereign's weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position. Nevertheless, \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's LTFC IDR remains on Stable Outlook, reflecting our view that the Turkish authorities will prioritise support for the bank (as for TKYB) compared with other Turkish banks with SRFs, given its policy role and smaller size relative to the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  calculates that the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 's net international on-balance sheet FC reserves fell to USD20 billion at end-March 2020 from USD37 billion at end-2019. Furthermore, when adjusting this figure for Turkish banks' FC swap transactions with the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  (but adding back more stable Treasury placements at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), we calculate the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 's net FC reserves to be materially lower, at about USD9 billion at end-March 2020. We estimate this position again deteriorated significantly in April 2020. This implies a marked reduction in the ability of the authorities to provide FC liquidity support to banks in case of need.\par \par \uc2 The sovereign's net FC reserves position should, however, be considered in light of Turkey's limited short-term external debt requirements. In addition, access to external funding markets could underpin Turkey's ability to provide FC support to the banks in case of need, depending on market conditions. The government was able to raise FC debt in 1Q20, and recently agreed on an extended US dollar swap line with Qatar.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's SRF is revised downwards by one notch to 'B' from 'B+', thereby widening its notching from the sovereign rating. Its Support Rating is affirmed at '4'. As a result, its LTFC IDR is now driven by its VR of 'b+'. The wider notching of TSKB's SRF than that of TKYB (BB-) and \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  (B+) reflects the bank's private ownership (by Turkiye Is Bankasi Group; B+/Negative).\par \par \uc2 At the 'B' level, TSKB's SRF is equal with the SRFs of state-owned, systemically important commercial banks. This reflects our view that TSKB is of strategic importance to the Turkish authorities, given its policy role, expertise in development lending (particularly in the energy sector), access to long-term funding from development financial institutions (largely under Treasury guarantee) and strong record of execution.\par \par \uc2 At end-1Q20, Treasury-guaranteed funding made up a high 83% of TKYB's total funding - the remaining was USD400 million of non-guaranteed funding (equal to about 17% of total funding)- and 51% in the case of TSKB. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Treasury-guaranteed funding made up 68% of \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's total funding.\par \par \uc2 The 'BB-' Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDRs of TKYB, \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  and TSKB (one notch above both \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's and TSKB's LTFC IDRs), which are equalised with the sovereign LTLC IDR, are driven by state support and reflect the sovereign's greater ability to provide support in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). TSKB has only limited LC liabilities.\par \par \uc2 VR OF TSKB\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR of TSKB is now driven by its VR, which reflects the concentration of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the high-risk and volatile Turkish operating environment. The bank's capital and FC liquidity buffers are only adequate for its risk profile and risks to the SCP - which were already significant after the Turkish lira depreciation in 2018 and economic slowdown in 2H18-2019 - have been heightened by the virus outbreak and lockdown measures.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts Turkey's GDP will contract 2% in 2020 - representing a 5.7pp decline compared with our March forecast - followed by a subsequent sharp recovery (4.9% growth) in 2021. Our baseline scenario assumes that containment measures will be gradually unwound in 2H20, allowing for recovery in the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 However, we see material downside risks to our forecasts, given uncertainty over the duration and ultimate impact of the crisis in Turkey. A second wave of infections or a prolongation of the lockdown period that leads to a sharper-than-expected weakening in economic growth and an ensuing weaker recovery in 2021 would add to existing pressures on TSKB's credit profile and could result in a rating downgrade.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy measures and fiscal support for the private sector and financial markets - including the latest \uc2 CBRT\uc2  interest rate cuts and the expanded Credit Guarantee Fund (CGF) (whereby loans to SMEs and certain retail borrowers disbursed under the facility are covered by a Turkish Treasury guarantee up to a certain non-performing loan (NPL) cap) - should support borrowers' repayment capacity and the bank's ability to grow to a degree, despite challenging market conditions. In addition, regulatory forbearance measures will provide uplift to the bank's reported asset quality, capital and performance metrics over the short-term.\par \par \uc2 Key forbearance measures include a change in the definition of impaired loans (to overdue by 180+ days, from 90+ days) and temporary moratoriums on loans falling due in 2Q20. In addition, the bank can use end-2019 exchange rates to calculate FC risk-weighted assets (RWA) until end-2020, while also suspending mark-to-market losses on securities through equity in its capital-adequacy calculation. Banks can also apply a zero risk-weighting to FC government exposures.\par \par \uc2 We expect TSKB's underlying asset quality to deteriorate and impairments to rise despite these policy measures, given the severity and scale of the current downturn, although to what extent will ultimately depend on the duration of the crisis and the pace of recovery. In the short-term, the bank's reported asset-quality metrics will benefit from forbearance, although this may ultimately serve only to delay recognition of problematic exposures, in our view.\par \par \uc2 22 May 2020 14:44 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turkey's 3 -2-\par \par \uc2 The bank's NPL ratio has risen since early 2018, albeit from historically low levels. It was still low at 3.4% at end-1Q20, versus the sector average of 5%. However, Stage 2 loans, which are largely restructured, have also increased and amounted to a further 10.2% of loans at end-1Q20.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks have increased due to the weak growth outlook and lira depreciation given the bank's significant FC loan exposure (equal to a high 90% of gross loans at end-1Q20, versus the sector average of 36%) and that not all borrowers are fully hedged. Credit risks are also exacerbated by single-name risk, particularly in project-finance lending, which comprises long-term, FC-denominated exposures. However, as the loans are slowly amortising, asset-quality problems will feed through only gradually.\par \par \uc2 Exposures to the troubled energy and construction/real estate sectors are additional significant sources of risk, as these have come under pressure from the weak growth outlook, market illiquidity (real estate), lira depreciation (as loans are frequently in FC but revenue in lira) and weak energy prices (energy lending). Energy-sector lending (45% of total loans) is largely related to renewable projects that are covered by a government-guaranteed feed-in tariff set in FC, mitigating the credit risk. In project finance, loans are typically collateralised by the projects themselves and frequently also backed by sponsor- and government-guarantees.\par \par \uc2 The bank has posted below-sector average loan growth since end-2017, reflecting its more conservative risk appetite as the operating environment has deteriorated but also low demand and capital constraints. Lending rose 2% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted) in 2019 and remained flat in 1Q20. It is not budgeting for significant growth over the near-term.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's profitability metrics remain generally reasonable despite having weakened and the bank outperformed the sector in operating profit/RWA in 2019. Performance has been underpinned by its reasonable net interest margin - reflecting access to low-cost Treasury guaranteed funding and exposure predominantly to FC (as opposed to the more volatile lira) interest rates - strong cost control and manageable, but increasing, loan impairment charges. Profitability is likely to be muted in 2020 given low growth, but could deteriorate significantly in case of a marked weakening in asset quality. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap costs can also weigh on the net interest margin (NIM).\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation has come under pressure from the lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs). TSKB's CET1 ratio fell to 11.6% at end-1Q20, from 12.1% at end-2019, which represents only a moderate buffer over the minimum regulatory requirement of 7%. Its total capital ratio is stronger at 17.1%, supported by FC-denominated tier 2 capital, which provides a partial hedge against the lira depreciation. We estimate that forbearance measures provided an uplift of 130bp to the bank's end-1Q20 CET1 ratio.\par \par \uc2 Potential asset-quality deterioration represents a risk to the bank's capital position in light of its largely unreserved Stage 2 loans, high FC lending and single-name risk. Reserve coverage of NPLs is low at 34%, but rises to 53% including free provisions. Pre-impairment operating profit (1Q20: equal to about 5% of average gross loans) provides a solid buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 TSKB is fully wholesale-funded, primarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and with a significant portion guaranteed by Treasury, ensuring access to long-term development-related funding. It is unique among Turkish banks in having a liability structure that is significantly longer-term than its asset structure. The maturity profile of the bank's funding base is reasonably diversified.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity consists mainly of cash and placements with foreign and domestic banks and deliverable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  views TSKB's FC liquidity as only adequate.The bankrolled-over its USD350 million Eurobond in January, which it replaced with a USD400 million Eurobond with a five-year maturity. The bank also redeemed another USD350 million Eurobond in April. At end-1Q20 available FC liquid assets covered about 70% of short-term wholesale funding liabilities over a 12-month horizon, including unused but committed funding lines from development finance institutions, this rose to about 110%.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Ratings (NRs) of all three banks reflects our view that their creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF TSKB\par \par \uc2 TSKB's subordinated debt rating is notched down from the VR anchor rating. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has downgraded the subordinated notes' rating of TSKB to 'B-' from 'B', and removed it from Under Criteria Observation (UCO). This is in line with the change in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's baseline notching from the VR for loss-severity to two notches from one notch for such instruments, reflecting our expectation of poor recoveries in case of default.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 TSKB\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 -The most immediate downside rating sensitivity for TSKB's LTFC IDR is the economic and financial market fallout of the pandemic, given the negative implications for its asset quality, earnings, capitalisation and funding and liquidity profile.\par \par \uc2 -In the short-term, support packages and government stimulus implemented by the authorities will mitigate operating-environment pressures, while regulatory forbearance will also reduce the impact on its reported financial metrics. Nevertheless, further marked deterioration in the operating environment - in particular relating to lira depreciation, the growth outlook and external funding-market access - could lead to a VR downgrade. The more protracted and deeper the economic shock, the greater the risk to its credit profile and rating.\par \par \uc2 -Greater-than-expected deterioration in underlying asset quality or erosion of capital or FC liquidity buffers that leads to pressure on liquidity and funding could lead to a VR downgrade.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 -The Outlook on TSKB's LTFC IDR could be revised to Stable if both (1) economic conditions stabilise, thereby supporting its earnings, asset quality, capital position and funding stability; and (2) \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of a further marked deterioration in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, and therefore of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system, has abated.\par \par \uc2 -An upgrade of the bank's ratings is unlikely in the near-term given the Negative Outlook.\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATINGS (SRS), SUPPORT RATING FLOORS, NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The banks' SRs could be downgraded and SRFs revised down if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support for these banks in FC from the Turkish authorities. In the case of TKYB and \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  this would also drive a downgrade of the banks' LTFC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 In addition, TKYB's SRF could be revised down by one notch and its LTFC IDR downgraded, if its proportion of non-guaranteed funding increases materially - particularly if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes this to be indicative of a weakening in TKYB's policy role - or if there is a sharp increase in its balance sheet size. The ratings of \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  and TSKB are also sensitive to a weakening in their policy roles.\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDRs of all three banks could be downgraded if the Turkish sovereign is downgraded, if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes the sovereign's propensity to support the banks has reduced (not \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's base case) or if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  sees an increased likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in LC. Conversely, these ratings could be upgraded in case of a sovereign upgrade.\par \par \uc2 All three banks' National Ratings are sensitive to changes in their LTLC IDRs and also their relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF TSKB\par \par \uc2 TSKB's subordinated debt rating is primarily sensitive to a change in its VR anchor rating.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's financial spreadsheets for TSKB that has impacted \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's core and complimentary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 TKYB's and \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's ratings are driven by the banks' respective SRFs, which are linked to sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 The highest level of ESG credit relevance, if present, is a score of 3. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity(ies), either due to their nature or to the way in which they are being managed by the entity(ies). For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 22 May 2020 14:44 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turkey's 3 -3-\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Kalkinma ve Yatirim Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AAA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 3\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; BB-\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Ihracat Kredi Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AAA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; RO:Neg\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Support Rating Floor Revision; B\par \par \uc2 ----subordinated; Long Term Rating; Downgrade; B-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ahmet Kilinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1272\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Liddell,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1008\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Cem Bilgin,\par \par \uc2 Senior Analyst\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1230\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ahmet Kilinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1272\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com.\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Applicable Criteria\par \par \uc2 Bank Rating Criteria (pub. 28 Feb 2020) (including rating assumption sensitivity)\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10110041"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10110041 }}}\par \par \uc2 National Scale Ratings Criteria (pub. 18 Jul 2018)\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10038626"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10038626 }}}\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/dodd-frank-disclosure/10123531"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/dodd-frank-disclosure/10123531 }}}\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Status\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10123531#solicitation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10123531#solicitation }}}\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Status\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10123531#endorsement_status"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10123531#endorsement_status }}}\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Policy\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/regulatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/regulatory }}}\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: HTTPS://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS. IN ADDITION, THE FOLLOWING HTTPS://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/RATING-DEFINITIONS-DOCUMENT DETAILS FITCH'S RATING DEFINITIONS FOR EACH RATING SCALE AND RATING CATEGORIES, INCLUDING DEFINITIONS RELATING TO DEFAULT. PUBLISHED RATINGS, CRITERIA, AND METHODOLOGIES ARE AVAILABLE FROM THIS SITE AT ALL TIMES. FITCH'S CODE OF CONDUCT, CONFIDENTIALITY, CONFLICTS OF INTEREST, AFFILIATE FIREWALL, COMPLIANCE, AND OTHER RELEVANT POLICIES AND PROCEDURES ARE ALSO AVAILABLE FROM THE CODE OF CONDUCT SECTION OF THIS SITE. DIRECTORS AND SHAREHOLDERS RELEVANT INTERESTS ARE AVAILABLE AT HTTPS://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/SITE/REGULATORY. FITCH MAY HAVE PROVIDED ANOTHER PERMISSIBLE SERVICE TO THE RATED ENTITY OR ITS RELATED THIRD PARTIES. DETAILS OF THIS SERVICE FOR RATINGS FOR WHICH THE LEAD ANALYST IS BASED IN AN EU-REGISTERED ENTITY CAN BE FOUND ON THE ENTITY SUMMARY PAGE FOR THIS ISSUER ON THE FITCH WEBSITE.\par \par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020 by \uc2 Fitch Ratings, Inc\uc2  ., \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\uc2  . and its subsidiaries. 33 Whitehall Street, NY, NY 10004. Telephone: 1-800-753-4824, (212) 908-0500. Fax: (212) 480-4435. Reproduction or retransmission in whole or in part is prohibited except by permission. All rights reserved. In issuing and maintaining its ratings and in making other reports (including forecast information), \uc2 Fitch\uc2  relies on factual information it receives from issuers and underwriters and from other sources \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes to be credible. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  conducts a reasonable investigation of the factual information relied upon by it in accordance with its ratings methodology, and obtains reasonable verification of that information from independent sources, to the extent such sources are available for a given security or in a given jurisdiction. The manner of \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's factual investigation and the scope of the third-party verification it obtains will vary depending on the nature of the rated security and its issuer, the requirements and practices in the jurisdiction in which the rated security is offered and sold and/or the issuer is located, the availability and nature of relevant public information, access to the management of the issuer and its advisers, the availability of pre-existi