\par\par \b \uc2 Big Foreign Debts Weigh on Turkish Banks -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Caitlin Ostroff and Avantika Chilkoti \par \uc2 943 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (May 21, 2020).\par \par \uc2 Turkey's economy, under pressure from the coronavirus pandemic, has a major vulnerability: its local banks' foreign debt.\par \par \uc2 Investors are worried Turkish banks won't have enough euros and dollars to hand and will struggle to raise funds overseas as a wall of debt comes due over the next year.\par \par \uc2 Turkish banks' funding vulnerability has been highlighted in recent weeks as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowed dollars and euros from local lenders. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a sharp decline in the lira against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 That leaves the banks in a potentially precarious position if they need dollars and euros to repay loans, and could lead to a credit crunch in Turkey if some lenders are forced to pare their balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 "If I was to pick one area of the economy that could cause a fairly bad economic situation to get even worse, it would be the banking sector," said \uc2 Jason Tuvey\uc2 , senior emerging market economist at Capital Economics. "It runs the risk of an economic crisis becoming a broader financial crisis within Turkey."\par \par \uc2 The sector is arguably in a worse position than it was during Turkey's 2018 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. While external debt has edged lower in recent years, the banks still have nearly $81 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt coming due in the next year, equivalent to more than 10% of the nation's total economic output. That compares with an estimated $30 billion that the banks have in reserves at \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having drawn down on those in recent years to repay debts.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  lowered the ratings outlook to negative on several Turkish banks, including state-owned giants T.C. \uc2 Ziraat Bankasi\uc2  and \uc2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi\uc2 . The credit-rating company cited the nation's weaker foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector as factors.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banks will likely pay a premium to extend their debt, said Nikolay Markov, a senior economist at Pictet Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "The risks have significantly increased," he said. "If the situation deteriorates further in the coming months, then that would be very negative for Turkey as it's already in a weak position."\par \par \uc2 The banks are seen as having adequate capital buffers to cope with a rise in defaults among its customers because of the coronavirus. But the banks' overseas borrowing costs -- as reflected in the spreads, or extra yield demanded on five-year dollar-denominated bonds over U.S. Treasurys -- climbed in early April to over 10 percentage points, or close to their 2018 peak, before easing to 6.7 points Tuesday, according to \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lenders have survived a series of rough patches, including the 2008-09 financial crisis, the taper tantrum of 2013 as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  prepared to unwind its balance sheet and a 40% depreciation in the lira in mid-2018.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also managed to secure access to external funding. On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had reached a deal with its counterpart in Qatar to increase the overall limit of an existing bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement to $15 billion from $5 billion.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, banks have sharply increased lending to households and companies in response to pressure from President Recep Tayyip Erdo an to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 The trouble is, Turkish people have generally proven reluctant to keep their savings in lira because of steep inflation. So banks borrowed dollars and euros and converted them into lira to fund domestic loans.\par \par \uc2 On the surface, Turkey's banks appear to have ample dollar funding. Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, which account for around half the liabilities in the banking system, are larger than foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans.\par \par \uc2 However, banks have also borrowed heavily overseas, largely through syndicated loans from foreign lenders. The financial sector's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing was more than four times its borrowing in lira at the end of December, according to data from the Institute of International Finance. That could leave the banking system vulnerable when overseas debts come due, especially as the lira loses value.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pressure: the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing dollars from commercial banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. This could erode banks' access to the surplus \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits they hold, according to \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at the New York-based \uc2 Council on Foreign Relations\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with a temporary ban on lira trading by three western banks, have reversed the lira's slide in the past two weeks. But many worry such a rebound is temporary. The lira is still down 13% against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed interest rates from 24% to 8.75% over the last 10 months, and is expected to continue the monetary easing Thursday. With the interest rate already below inflation, that is making holding the lira less attractive for investors. It is also making domestic lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  less profitable for the banks.\par \par \uc2 Write to Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com and Avantika Chilkoti at Avantika.Chilkoti@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications A chart published with this article shows the Turkish banking system's external, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheet. An earlier version of the chart incorrectly double-counted short-term liabilities in the total of external liabilities.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 21, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200521eg5l000h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
