\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Turkey FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Backfire, Says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2020\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0901 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should urgently stop using indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail the lira's decline as it could run out of resources, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "Trying to stem lira depreciation by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a limited \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve pool, and muddying the waters further by asking commercial banks to sell down their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  balances are rather questionable," Commerzbank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Thu Lan Nguyen says. Such a strategy "can only work if the problem is very short-term" and this is "not at all guaranteed," she says. USD/TRY rises 0.1% to 6.9481.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT - A global recovery in the second half of the year could be slow if the lifting of coronavirus restrictions is uncoordinated, Oxford Economics's John Payne says. "Bottlenecks in industrial sector supply chains could exacerbate other ongoing constraints, such as social distancing, in the industrial sector," Payne says. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  contributes more than 30% to global core intermediate goods exports and this dwarfs the importance of China and U.S. in supply chains, meaning shutdowns in the bloc would cause severe disruptions. With widespread lockdowns delivering a major hit to domestic economies, OE predicts an unprecedented plunge in global activity in the first half of 2020. Regarding the rebound, OE expects global gross domestic product to recover quicker than industrial production. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT - \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  is advising clients to increasingly consider adding in corporate bonds that are not eligible for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's corporate bond-buying program, known as the corporate sector purchase program. Non-eligible bonds trade at higher spreads in asset swap terms that CSPP-eligible bonds, but that gap is unlikely to widen. "We do not see much scope for the gap between CSPP ineligible and eligible names to grow further from elevated levels and would increasingly consider adding in ineligible names," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's credit strategist Cem Keltek. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT - Japan's GDP risks contracting further after Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  expanded a state of emergency to the whole country until May 6, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. A worsening growth outlook for Europe and the U.S. is another reason JPM expects Japan's economic contraction to deepen. It now expects real GDP to shrink 35% on quarter in 2Q, compared with an earlier forecast of 17% contraction, and projects 2020 GDP to contract 7.3%. Japan is also likely to return to deflation, JPM says. All this means more macroeconomic policies are probably needed for the country's economy and inflation to rebound, JPM adds. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0826 GMT - Despite a recent tightening in European corporate bond spreads, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  warns that fallen angel risk--when investment-grade companies are downgraded to junk--and default risk continue to pose an imminent threat to bondholders. Even after "the remarkable recovery in credit spreads since 23 March notwithstanding... the path to economic normality remains clouded and fallen angel and default risks still loom large," says Cem Keltek, a credit strategist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . "We would continue to strategically add exposure and still prefer strong Noncyclicals and Financials with multi notch buffers to high-yield." (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT - Early signs of the coronavirus curve flattening and a partial easing of lockdowns provide hope for the eurozone economy but the damage has been done, indicating extremely poor growth for the year, \uc2 Ned Davis Research\uc2  says. "The few pieces of data we have so far show an economy that was completely pulverized in March," the research firm says. The fiscal and monetary response has been unprecedented, it adds, noting that "compared to past quantitative easing programs from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , this takes the cake in terms of its breadth and depth." However, this stimulus doesn't necessarily imply a quick return to prepandemic GDP and \uc2 Ned Davis Research\uc2  expects a U-shaped recovery at best, as downside risks remain elevated. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT - Sterling traders seem relaxed about the U.K.'s refusal to extend the Brexit transition period as they are more focused on the coronavirus pandemic in the short term, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The application for an extension to the transition period beyond the end of 2020 needs to be submitted by July so pressure from Brexit on sterling "should still be limited for now," Commerzbank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Thu Lan Nguyen says. "However, unless the government caves in beforehand and postpones the negotiations about a free trade agreement with the EU , pressure on sterling is going to mount in June at the latest due to the increased risk of a hard Brexit." GBP/USD falls 0.2% to 1.2436 and EUR/GBP is flat at 0.8705.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 GMT - Sweden's Verisure has become the first high yield-rated European company to raise funds in the debt market since February 20, when the coronavirus-led selloff started. This deal and continued inflows over the last three weeks show "returning confidence" to credit markets, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's credit strategist Cem Keltek. The security company opted for a floating rate note for its EUR200 million deal.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  increases its forecast for gross government bond issuance by the eurozone's 11 largest issuers to EUR1.011 trillion. "SocGen Ups Forecast for Eurozone-11 Gross Govt Bond Issuance Due to Coronavirus -- Market Talk," at 0710 GMT, misstated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first sentence. SocGen increases its forecast to EUR1.011 trillion, not $1.011 trillion.\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT - Cautious risk-on sentiment prevails in eurozone government bond markets with hopes that European countries can join forces more effectively to cope with the effect of the coronavirus. This allows peripheral bond yields to drop amid stronger appetite, and causes core bond yields to edge higher in indication of less demand for havens. "The next key point for eurozone government bond markets is the heads of state meeting on April 23," \uc2 Danske\uc2 's senior analyst Aila Mihr says. There is no government bond issuance in the eurozone on Friday but Belgium is scheduled to announce the target volume for its bond auction on Monday. The 10-year Italian BTP yield is trading almost seven basis points lower at 1.776%, while the 10-year Bund yield is up half a basis point at minus 0.467%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT - Singapore's "circuit breaker" period from April 7 to May 4 could hurt the local economy further, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says, cutting its 2020 GDP forecast to minus 6% from 2.8% previously. During the "circuit breaker" period, close to 25%-30% of the country's economy could come to a standstill, \uc2 Citi\uc2  estimates. When the restrictions imposed by the "circuit breaker" are lifted, the technical rebound in May-June activity may be limited by continued social distancing and soft external demand, leading to a possible 20%-25% on-quarter contraction in 2Q GDP. Every month of "circuit breaker" extension will probably further reduce 2020 GDP growth by 2.0-2.5 percentage points, \uc2 Citi\uc2  estimates. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT - The euro rises against a weaker dollar on improved market sentiment but further gains are likely to be restricted by concerns about EU divisions over the economic response to coronavirus, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "The hesitant handling of the coronavirus in Europe has, in our view, exposed the lack of institutional willpower to keep the region together; this in our view caps euro upside," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says, adding that it expects EUR/USD to trade around 1.09 in one to three months and 1.07 in six to 12 months. EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.0857 as demand for the safe haven dollar falls following encouraging news of a potential treatment for coronavirus and after Germany and the U.S. announced plans to ease lockdowns. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 17, 2020 05:01 ET (09:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200417eg4h000vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
