\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's IDR at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2020\par 18:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 14 Feb 2020 12:14 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's IDR at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-14 February 2020:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed QNB Finansbank A.S.'s Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+'. The Outlook is Stable. At the same time the bank's Viability Rating (VR) is affirmed at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 The support-driven LTFC IDRs of the bank's subsidiaries QNB Finans Finansal Kiralama A.S. (Finansleasing) and QNB Finans Faktoring A.S. (QNB Faktoring), which are equalised with that of their parent, have also been affirmed.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is detailed below.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finans Finansal Kiralama A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 QNB Finans Faktoring A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); RO:Sta\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 VR, LTFC IDR, NATIONAL RATING, SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  are driven by its standalone creditworthiness, as expressed by its 'b+' VR, reflecting the concentration of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the challenging Turkish operating environment, which exposes it to market volatility and political and geopolitical uncertainty. However, short-term operating environment risks have partly abated given Turkey's progress in stabilising and rebalancing the economy, reducing downside risks to the bank's credit profile.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook on the LTFC IDR reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that at the current rating level risks to the bank's credit profile from the operating environment and asset-quality pressures should be manageable given \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's capital, profitability and liquidity buffers.\par \par \uc2 The VR also considers the bank's moderate franchise (end-2019: 4% share of banking-sector assets) and ensuing limited competitive advantages. The bank provides a mix of services to corporate and commercial customers - a segment where it is looking to increase its market shares - small and medium-sized companies (SMEs, which are highly sensitive to the macro outlook) and retail customers. As a key subsidiary of \uc2 Qatar National Bank\uc2  (\uc2 QNB\uc2 , A+/Stable) we also see increasing integration with the wider group in its risk management and control environment and policies and procedures.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  posted above-sector-average loan growth in 2019 (11.8% on an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted basis), driven mainly by local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending to corporate, commercial and retail segments, while its growth appetite in the high-risk SME segment was lower than peers'. Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) loan growth was slower, but above sector-average despite exchange-rate volatility. The bank continues to target above-sector-average loan growth in 2020, balanced mainly between retail and business banking. Over the medium- to long-term, management guides that this will come from its primary focus on corporate banking.\par \par \uc2 The bank's asset quality weakened in 2019, due to loan seasoning and pressure notably on its SME and retail portfolios under challenging conditions over the last two years. The IFRS 9 Stage 3 /gross loans ratio rose to 7% at end-2019 (sector average: 5.3%), but is distorted by regular non-performing loan (NPL) sales and /write-offs (2019: equal to 77bp of gross loans), as for many banks in the sector. Stage 2 loans were also fairly high (9.7% of gross loans at end-2019, up from 9.2% at end-2018), of which nearly two-thirds were restructured. Total loan loss allowance coverage of impaired loans was 100% at end-2019, slightly above the sector average (95%). Management aims to increase this level further in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Credit risks at the bank are heightened by high, albeit below sector-average, FC lending (end-2019: 32% of gross loans), given the potential impact of local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) depreciation on often weakly hedged borrowers' ability to service debt, and by its exposure to the high-risk SME segment (end-2019: 30% of gross loans). Exposure to the construction (9M19: 6%) and energy (4%) sectors - which have come under pressure in the challenging operating environment - are additional credit risk factors. Single-name concentration risk at the bank is moderate relative to capital.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's exposure to project finance is also significant; it amounted to around one-fifth of gross loans at end-9M19 (1.5x \uc2 Fitch\uc2  core capital (\uc2 FCC\uc2  )) and mainly comprised long-term, FC-denominated, slowly amortising energy project (16% of the portfolio) and infrastructure (64%). Credit risk in the book is partly mitigated by around 60% of project-finance loans being covered by state guarantees on debt and revenue, or feed-in tariffs set in US dollars in the case of some renewable energy projects.\par \par \uc2 We believe that further downside risks to the bank's asset quality is reduced to some extent, as for the sector, given the improving growth outlook in Turkey, the lower interest-rate environment - following sharp lira rate cuts since end-1H19 - and lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's profitability metrics remained above sector-average in 2019. It reported an operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) of 2.2%. Performance has been underpinned by its above-sector- average net interest margin (reflecting its focus on higher-yielding, more high-risk segments), above-sector-average loan growth and moderate impairment charges to date (2019: equal to 36% of pre-impairment profit). However, it remains sensitive to asset-quality weakening, while competitive pricing on loans could erode margins, despite lower lira funding costs. Cost-efficiency ratios have improved steadily in recent years (end-2019: non-interest expense-to-average total assets of 2.3%), albeit slightly underperforming the sector's due to \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's lack of scale.\par \par \uc2 Core capitalisation is only moderate given the bank's risk profile (end-2019: \uc2 FCC\uc2  ratio of 10.1%) and growth appetite. Its total regulatory capital ratio was more comfortable (end-2019: 15.2% versus the 12% recommended regulatory minimum), supported by FC subordinated debt from \uc2 QNB\uc2 . The \uc2 QNB\uc2  debt provides a partial hedge against potential lira depreciation. Buffers over Basel III capital requirements are moderate.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation remains sensitive, as for the sector, to lira depreciation, via inflation of FC RWAs and asset-quality weakening. However, pre-impairment profit (2019: equal to 4.4% of average loans) provides a reasonable buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 The bank is mainly deposit funded (2019: 65% of total funding). Around half of customer deposits are in FC, broadly in line with the sector's. It has an above-sector-average loans/deposit ratio (125% at end-2019), however, reflecting reliance on FC wholesale funding (end-2019: 30% of total funding).\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity is adequate and was broadly sufficient to cover short-term liabilities maturing within one year as at end-9M19, meaning the bank should be able to cope with a short-lived market closure. Refinancing risk is also mitigated by potential liquidity support from \uc2 QNB\uc2 . Available FC liquidity comprises cash and interbank placements (including balances with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  ), maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and government securities. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure from prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 The bank's LTFC IDR is also underpinned, at the 'B+' level, by potential institutional support ('4' Support Rating) from \uc2 QNB\uc2 . \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of support is based on the bank's strategic importance to \uc2 QNB\uc2 , integration, role within the wider group and shared branding. However, its LTFC IDR is capped at 'B+' on the basis of support, one notch below Turkey's 'BB-' rating, reflecting our view that, in case of a marked deterioration in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector would be higher than that of a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 The bank's Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is however rated one notch above the LTFC IDR at 'BB-', as we see a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in LC than in FC. The Stable Outlook on the LTLC IDR consequently mirrors the Outlook on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's high external funding requirement creates a significant incentive for the authorities to retain market access and avoid capital controls. However, in case of marked deterioration in the country's external finances, some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that might impede banks' ability to service FC obligations would become more likely.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Rating reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers' has not changed.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 14 Feb 2020 12:14 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's IDR -2-\par \par \uc2 The support-driven ratings of Finansleasing and QNB Faktoring are equalised with those of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , reflecting their strategic importance to and close integration with their parent (including the sharing of risk-assessment systems, customers, branding and management resources).\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 VR, IDRS, NATIONAL RATING, SUPPORT RATING AND SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs, Support Rating and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to Turkey's sovereign rating and changes in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. An increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which could be driven by a sovereign downgrade or a marked deterioration in Turkey's external finances, could result in a downgrade of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's ratings, whereas a reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, likely linked to a sovereign upgrade or an improvement in external finances, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of \uc2 QNB\uc2 's propensity to support \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  could also result in a downgrade, but would only result in a downgrade of the LTFC IDR and senior debt rating if at the same time \uc2 Fitch\uc2  also downgrades the bank's VR.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded in case of a marked deterioration in the operating environment, as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, domestic interest rates, economic growth prospects, and external funding market access. This may also result from a weakening of the bank's FC liquidity, most likely due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, without this being offset by shareholder support; or greater-than-expected pressure on asset quality or high loan growth that leads to a marked erosion of the bank's capital position.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term, in light of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's moderate franchise, asset- quality pressures, only moderate core capitalisation and the 'BB-' LTFC IDR of the Turkish sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to a change in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of support leading to a change in \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's LTLC IDR. It is also sensitive to a change in the entity's creditworthiness relative to other rated Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Finansleasing and QNB Faktoring are sensitive to changes in the Long-Term IDRs of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's financial spreadsheets of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  that has impacted core and complimentary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan that was classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of 3. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or to the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on our ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Behruz Ismailov,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +49 69 768076 116\par \par \uc2 Fitch Deutschland GmbH\par \par \uc2 Neue Mainzer Strasse 46 - 50\par \par \uc2 Frankfurt am Main D-60311\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Li