\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  to Prove Futile -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2020\par 17:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1113 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest efforts to stabilize the lira will most likely "prove futile," says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  , which expects USD/TRY to rise to 7.50 by year-end from 6.0103 currently. Turkey's banking regulator on Sunday cut the limit for domestic banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap, spot and forward transactions with foreign entities to 10% of a bank's equity in an attempt to reduce bets against the lira. "The longer that policymakers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the lira, the greater the risk of a disorderly adjustment," Capital Economics economist \uc2 Jason Tuvey\uc2  says. Authorities will "ultimately have to loosen their grip on the lira," which will weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 ET - The Turkish lira falls against the dollar after briefly erasing Friday's losses following new government curbs on banks which are aimed at limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation by foreign investors. Turkey's banking regulator on Sunday cut the limit for domestic banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap, spot and forward transactions with foreign entities to 10% of a bank's equity, sending USD/TRY as low as 5.9763 Monday before turning back up again, last trading at 6.0099. "Imposing liquidity restrictions is a short-term solution that cannot be a substitute for comprehensive structural reforms, which are urgently required to improve sentiment towards the lira and local assets in a sustainable way," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  analyst \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2  says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 ET - The euro falls against the safe-haven dollar due to concerns about the economic impact of China's coronavirus outbreak, ING says. "The coronavirus and its impact on global supply chains is seen as a much bigger issue for Germany than for the U.S., thus EUR/USD is under pressure," ING analyst \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2  says. Meanwhile, investors will be closely watching data on eurozone industrial production on Tuesday and German GDP on Friday following "truly appalling" German factory orders figures Thursday, he says. ING sees EUR/USD reaching a low of 1.0900 this week. It is last down 0.1% at 1.0934. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0945 ET - The apparent lack of policy coordination between the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and governments over higher government spending and fiscal stimulus is a worry for Brooks Macdonald. "Despite renewed calls from \uc2 ECB\uc2  Presidents past and present to seek increased government spending and fiscal stimulus, we are concerned by the apparent lack of policy co-ordination, with the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and the \uc2 German government\uc2  effectively trying to drive the Eurozone economy with one foot on the accelerator and one foot on the brake," says investment strategist Matthew Cady. The asset manager sees limited room for manoeuvre for the \uc2 ECB\uc2  given negative interest rates, Cady says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is "mindful" of the adverse impact of pushing policy deeper into negative territory on an already weakened banking sector in the eurozone, he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 ET - The coronavirus outbreak is costing container lines $350M per week in lost volumes, with more than 350,000 boxes removed from global trade since the disease broke out last month, according to Denmark-based, maritime data provider Sea-Intelligence. At least 21 sailings from China to the Americas have been cancelled so far, along with 10 cancellations in the Asia to Europe trade loop. The epidemic will hit the shipping industry hard, with the majority of operators trying to stay above water, fighting trade disputes and a slowing global economy. (costas.paris@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 ET - The U.K. economy probably contracted in the fourth quarter due to heightened Brexit uncertainty ahead of December's general election, Daiwa says ahead of official GDP data due Tuesday. Daiwa expects GDP to fall by a quarterly 0.1% in the final three months of 2019 with household consu*mption and business investment "set to have remained very weak," compared with 0.4% growth in the third quarter. "While monthly data are expected to show some bounce back in activity in December--industrial production, services, construction and trade figures also due--and the end-October Brexit deadline will have distorted the demand profile, overall the U.K.'s economy is anticipated to have slowed in the final quarter of the year," Daiwa says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 ET - The WSJ Dollar Index, which measures the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of 16 others, slipped Monday to around 91.56 from 91.59. The decline followed a 1.15% rise in the index last week, its strongest one-week gain since August. (matt.wirz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 ET - The possible successors to Christian Democratic Union leader \uc2 Annegret Kramp-Karrenbauer\uc2  offer differing economic policy profiles, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's chief economist \uc2 Joerg Kraemer\uc2  says. Kramp-Karrenbauer said earlier Monday that she will renounce her candidacy for chancellor and resign as chairwoman of the center-right party. Among the candidates to succeed her, \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2  represents a more market-oriented policy course, \uc2 Armin Laschet\uc2  is considered less market-oriented and \uc2 Jens Spahn\uc2  would concentrate more on social policy, Kramer says. "However, it is at least as important whether the Greens ultimately decide to form a coalition with the CDU or, as in the federal states of Berlin or Bremen, seek a left-wing coalition, Kraemer says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - Investors are playing the safety trade this morning, exiting stocks and buying up Treasury bonds as concerns about fallout from the coronavirus mount. The 10-year Treasury yield was around 1.563% Monday from 1.578% Friday, according to data from \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (matt.wirz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 ET - Change in leadership for the conservative Christian Democratic Union in Germany will only have a modest impact on the party's policy outlook, economists at Berenberg say. Current leader \uc2 Annegret Kramp-Karrenbauer\uc2  said she will step down as head of the party and not run for chancellor after an election in the state of Thuringia with an aborted alliance between populists and conservatives caused a crisis within the party. Kramp-Karrenbauer was largely seen as prime contender for chancellorship and was \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  's chosen successor. \uc2 Armin Laschet\uc2 --head of German state North Rhine-Westphalia--and \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2  --who competed against Kramp-Karrenbauer in the last CDU leadership election and lost--are currently seen as the two favorites to replace her, analysts say. Other candidates, such as health minister \uc2 Jens Spahn\uc2  , could also be considered, according to analysts. (kim.richters@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - The coronavirus outbreak has come "at a particularly inopportune moment for the global economy," says \uc2 George Lagarias\uc2  , chief economist at accounting, tax and consulting firm \uc2 Mazars\uc2 . "After a year of delay in terms of recovery, in the past three months the global economy has shown signs of a pickup, mostly due to better data out of China," he says. Because that rebound was cyclical rather than structural, however, it was "fragile to events and externalities." Under a less shaky backdrop, a virus impact such as this would ordinarily only have a short-term effect on the economy, he says. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0810 ET - Fiscal and monetary policy, corporate and consumer sentiment, fund flows and valuation all support sustained U.K. equity outperformance, despite uncertainty created by the U.K.'s EU exit, says Jefferies. The brokerage says downside risks from policy mis-steps remain priced in, though positives aren't. It points to rising home-loan approvals, increased house prices and business surveys showing higher business optimism. "This is all before what is likely to be a highly expansionary (\uc2 U.K. government\uc2  ) budget statement on Mar. 11," Jefferies analysts say. "U.K. equities look significantly under-valued, with the FTSE 100 now at a 14% discount to the Stoxx Europe 600." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 10, 2020 11:13 ET (16:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200210eg2a002c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
