\par\par \b \uc2 Cold Turkey: investor exodus tests Erdogan's economic experiment\b0\par\par\uc2 1,057 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2020\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL (Reuters) - A run on the lira proved a pivotal moment for Turkey's financial markets in 2018, prompting action from Ankara that has tilted the economy inward and frightened off foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Turkish President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 , his government and his deputies have said the measures taken {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/378W6fq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/378W6fq}}}\uc2 , which sparked an exodus of foreign money, were needed to stabilize the economy and bolster belief in the lira, which has dropped 36% in two years.\par \par \uc2 But the impact on investment in what was once a darling of emerging markets has been dramatic. The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  estimates that net foreign direct investment, which fell 30% last year, will not regain 2018 levels until after 2021.\par \par \uc2 For import-dependent Turkey, some analysts say the danger is that this outflow could over time starve the Middle East's top economy of funds and stall Erdogan's recovery plans.\par \par \uc2 Former Turkish Treasury research associate Ugras Ulku, who now heads the Institute of International Finance's (IIF) regional Emerging Markets research, said that "tighter control on financial markets, risks including U.S. sanctions and Turkish borrowers' inability to access foreign funding" mean the country may miss out on foreign cash.\par \par \uc2 Overseas investors have largely steered clear of Turkey's sovereign bonds, despite a once-in-a-decade rally since May, while some players are reconsidering their strategies.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is considering selling its Turkish bank, sources familiar with the matter told Reuters last week, while \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  <C.N>, one of the top foreign banks in Turkey, transferred at least two \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates traders to London from Istanbul last year due to the uncertainty, a source with knowledge of the move said.\par \par \uc2 Citi and \uc2 HSBC\uc2  both declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The fallout has also led to losses for \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  <GS.N> and other foreign firms who were burnt in 2019 when Turkey directed its banks to withhold liquidity from a key London swap market to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , two bankers familiar with trading activity said.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The swap market move was one of dozens of dictats and regulations to defend the lira, from setting deposit rates at lenders to tapping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves.\par \par \uc2 New rules and regulations spiked to 3,800 in 2018 from 551 in 2007, a \uc2 World Bank\uc2  analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/bitstream/handle/10986/32634/Turkey-Economic-Monitor-Charting-a-New-Course.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/bitstream/handle/10986/32634/Turkey-Economic-Monitor-Charting-a-New-Course.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y}}}\uc2  shows.\par \par \uc2 Interest rate cuts, along with a surge in public spending and a push to get state-owned banks to lend more, have helped Turkey recover from recession. But a rapid uptick in lending in an already indebted economy and a widening budget deficit risk storing up trouble, ratings agencies and economists say.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's Treasury and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  each declined to comment. Erdogan's office was not available to comment.\par \par \uc2 A government effort to boost lending a year ago, by cutting the amount of interest Turkish savers could get from their deposit account, prompted some to keep their spare cash in dollars or euros, according to bankers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 State banks have since March tapped up to $32 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in buying up lira, a Reuters analysis of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet shows.\par \par \uc2 The state banks then re-deposit lira at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials and bankers with knowledge of this loop told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 An official at one state lender said Turkey's banks are now staffed by traders at all hours, part of what some call a "national team" ready to respond to any lira weakness.\par \par \uc2 Despite this support, the lira skidded 11% last year, with the biggest slide between March and May, as \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  began.\par \par \uc2 For a timeline of post-crisis \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , see: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://graphics.reuters.com/TURKEY-ECONOMY-FOREIGNERS/0100B4X32T8/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://graphics.reuters.com/\b  \highlight22TURKEY\b0 \highlight22 -ECONOMY-FOREIGNERS/0100B4X32T8/index.html}}}\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC: Foreigners turn away from Turkish assets - {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fingfx.thomsonreuters.com/gfx/editorcharts/TURKEY-ERDOGAN-MARKETS/0H001R87XBML/eikon.png"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://fingfx.thomsonreuters.com/gfx/editorcharts/TURKEY-ERDOGAN-MARKETS/0H001R87XBML/eikon.png}}}\par \par \uc2 SWAP MARKET\par \par \uc2 Last year non-residents sold a net total of $3.3 billion in Turkish bonds, compared to purchases of $7 billion in 2017, IIF data shows. That means local investors mostly profited as Turkey's benchmark 10-year yield dropped below 10% from 21% in May, thanks mostly to monetary easing and a drop in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The foreign flight is partly a response to the government's approach to markets after a 2017 referendum handed Erdogan wide-ranging authority over the courts, military and economy.\par \par \uc2 The clearest signal of an intensification of this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came days before nationwide local elections in March 2019 that would deal a blow to Erdogan's party, more than a dozen investors, bankers and Turkish officials told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 That was when Turkish state lenders suddenly cut short-term lira funding to the London swap market, which was popular for hedging and shorting, traders and bankers said, sending rates rocketing to 1,200%.\par \par \uc2 The move reversed a volatile drop in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but left big European and U.S. banks scrambling to cover positions and sell Turkish bonds, several sources have told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  was among the hardest hit, two of them said.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with Goldman's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that while the Wall Street firm faced the same tough conditions as others, it had not logged "material" losses.\par \par \uc2 Reuters could not confirm positions in the market, where bilateral trades are made privately.\par \par \uc2 The lira swap market, a source of funding for Turkish mortgages, has since collapsed, \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  data {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/markets/london-foreign-exchange-joint-standing-committee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/markets/london-\b  \highlight22foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight22 -joint-standing-committee}}}\uc2  shows, and several foreign investors say they no longer trust it.\par \par \uc2 Since the March episode, foreigners have dumped Turkish debt even while most other emerging markets benefited from a global hunt for yield. Data from Turkey's bank regulator shows they owned only 8.3% of the debt in mid-January, down from nearly 17% in early 2018.\par \par \uc2 "Subliminally they know that after all they have done they have beaten their guests and those guests will never come back," a Turkish investment adviser, speaking on condition of anonymity, said.\par \par \uc2 (This story corrects fourth paragraph to show Turkey is import-dependent)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ebru Tuncay; Writing by Jonathan Spicer; Editing by Alexander Smith) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-5T07:53:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200205eg2500ogl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
