\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Afrms Turkey Foreign And Local  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Rtngs; Otlk Stbl\b0\par\par\uc2 4,401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2020\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 31 Jan 2020 16:05 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Afrms Turkey Foreign And Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rtngs; Otlk Stbl\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- We estimate that last year Turkey posted its first current account \par surplus since the 2001 financial crisis, albeit largely at the cost of a \par contraction in real investment. \par      -- The authorities have launched multiple discretionary fiscal and credit \par measures, alongside the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Republic of Turkey\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) \par extensive monetary easing, intended to boost bank lending to consumers and \par companies. \par      -- In our view, the risk to this demand-led growth strategy is that \par Turkey's external and financial sector vulnerabilities could re-emerge if \par global financing shifts away from the current unusually benign market \par conditions. \par      -- We are affirming our unsolicited foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings on Turkey at \par 'B+/B' and our local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ratings at 'BB-/B'. The outlook remains stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On Jan. 31, 2020, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its unsolicited long-term \par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating on Turkey at 'B+' and its unsolicited \par long-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating at 'BB-'. The outlook is \par stable. \par  \par We affirmed the unsolicited short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign \par credit ratings at 'B'. \par  \par We also affirmed the unsolicited national scale ratings at 'trAA+/--/trA-1+'. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook reflects our baseline forecast that the Turkish economy and \par banking system will navigate existing challenges--for instance rolling over \par foreign funding and loan book quality--over the next year, helped by benign \par global financing conditions. Turkey's real growth should continue to recover \par during 2020, leading to a re-emergence of current account deficits, albeit \par more modest ones than previously. \par  \par We could lower our ratings on Turkey if we saw an increasing likelihood of \par systemic banking distress with potential negative repercussions for public \par finances. The potential for such distress could rise if \par higher-than-anticipated external deficits and rapid credit growth re-emerge. \par  \par We could consider an upgrade if the government successfully devised and \par implemented a credible and transparent economic adjustment program that \par bolsters confidence in Turkey's banks and its economy, while reducing \par balance-of-payments risks further and bringing about a sustained decline in \par inflation. \par  \par RATIONALE \par In our view, Turkey's short-term balance-of-payments risks have subsided \par because the banking system's external leverage has somewhat decreased, and \par banks have continued rolling over external funding. That said, the \par authorities' recent efforts to boost growth--including through credit \par stimulus--raise the risk of another bout of economic overheating. As in the \par past, Turkey's capacity to run external deficits and to refinance still-high \par external debt redemptions relies on global financing conditions, which have \par been unusually accommodating since 2018. \par  \par Following estimated 0.3% real growth in 2019, we think the Turkish economy is \par set to expand by a stronger 3.5% in 2020, followed by similar growth over the \par medium term. \par  \par Over the last two years, the Turkish authorities have introduced a series of \par discretionary fiscal and macro-prudential measures. \par  \par These include: \par      -- Front-loading and increasing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dividend payments to the \par budget (likely to be repeated in 2020); \par      -- Extending credit stimulus via state banks; \par      -- Lowering reserve requirements for banks with local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loan \par growth within a certain range, as well as various forms of capital relief; and \par      -- Delaying tax hikes on consumer-price-index sensitive products such as \par tobacco, and introducing tax cuts to benefit property sales and construction. \par  \par A comprehensive and coordinated approach has largely been absent, in our view, \par and some of the measures could ultimately prove distortionary or generate \par further imbalances. Moreover, it remains uncertain as to what the policy \par response could be should global financing conditions take a turn for the \par worse. \par  \par Positively, Turkey's comparatively low net general government debt still \par provides some fiscal headroom in the event of a hypothetical negative economic \par shock. \par  \par Our ratings on Turkey remain constrained by what we view as its weak \par institutions. We see limited checks and balances between government bodies, \par with power concentrated in the hands of the executive branch and so rendering \par future policy responses difficult to predict. Yet the outcome of local \par elections held in 2019 suggests that Turkey's political system retains a \par degree of competition. \par  \par Institutional and economic profile: The economy has returned to growth \par      -- Turkey's economy has gone through an adjustment phase following the \par substantial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility of August 2018, and has now returned to growth. \par      -- We forecast that real growth will average 3.3% annually over the \par medium term. \par      -- Policy and institutional risks remain elevated. \par  \par Following a period of adjustment after the substantial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility of \par August 2018, the Turkish economy has now returned to growth. It technically \par exited recession in first-quarter 2019 and has grown by an average of close to \par 1% in seasonally adjusted quarterly terms thereafter. We estimate that for \par full-year 2019 the economy grew by a marginal 0.3%, compared with our previous \par forecast of a small contraction in real output. Real investment contracted by \par an estimated 14%, while domestic consumption grew 1.6%; net export growth \par provided the main support both via import compression but also through export \par growth, including in the tourism sector, helped by a weaker Turkish lira (TRY) \par real effective exchange rate (REER). \par  \par We forecast that GDP growth should pick up to 3.5% in real terms in 2020, \par although there are uncertainties surrounding our forecast and there could be \par upside risks. As a baseline, we forecast a rebound in consumption by almost \par 4.0%, helped by a more stable exchange rate and moderating inflation. We also \par expect a gradual recovery of investment activity as credit conditions loosen \par on the back of previous aggressive \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy rate cuts, and as banking \par sector loan growth strengthens. \par  \par The authorities are targeting 5% real growth in 2020 as well as over the \par medium term, as communicated in the New Economic Program published in \par September 2019. We do not see this growth rate as achievable in the medium \par term, absent a return to large-scale credit and fiscal stimulus that could \par reignite economic imbalances. Our baseline growth forecasts are therefore \par lower than those of the authorities. We believe that lingering political and \par business environment risks will constrain investments--including FDI--and \par hamper growth. \par  \par We also consider that Turkey's improved economic performance and reduced \par immediate balance-of-payments risks has happened largely on the back of \par exceptionally accommodative foreign financing conditions. This has provided \par some breathing room and allowed Turkish banks to roll over the majority of \par their foreign debt and reduce leverage in an orderly fashion. The government's \par policy response has been and is still a rather ad hoc focus on relieving some \par symptoms of economic problems rather than implementing a comprehensive \par structural reform package. The authorities have recently implemented \par discretionary measures such as tax reductions in specific sectors and the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has given banks incentives to lend in Turkish lira, such as lower \par reserve requirements provided certain conditions are met. In our view, while \par these measures have had some success in providing short-term relief, they risk \par being distortionary and could result in renewed accumulation of imbalances in \par the longer term. \par  \par More broadly, Turkey's institutional arrangements have eroded substantially in \par recent years and are a major constraint on the sovereign ratings. In the June \par 2018 presidential and parliamentary elections, the president and the Adalet ve \par Kalkinma Partisi (AKP)-led coalition secured a victory that was the final \par chapter in Turkey's transition to an executive presidential system. We see \par limited checks and balances between government bodies, with power concentrated \par in the hands of the executive branch and so rendering future policy responses \par difficult to predict. \par  \par We also continue to see risks stemming from Turkey's international relations. \par Multiple points of contention remain in Turkey's relations with the U.S. One \par of the most pertinent frictions appears to be the Turkish government's \par decision to purchase S-400 surface-to-air missiles from Russia, which we \par understand arrived in Ankara last year. That raises the prospect of U.S. \par sanctions under the Countering America's Adversaries Through Sanctions Act, \par the severity of which are hard to predict. Our current forecasts are based on \par potential mild sanctions such as Turkey being excluded from purchasing F-35 \par military aircraft, alongside specific measures against selected companies and \par individuals. If sanctions turned out to be more far reaching--restricting \par banks' access to foreign funding for example--the consequences for Turkey's \par economy could be more pronounced. \par  \par Another flashpoint remains the Turkish military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Northern \par Syria--known as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Peace Spring. \par  \par Turkey's interactions with the EU  also remain complex. There are several \par points of cooperation, most notably the March 2016 refugee deal that is still \par being broadly implemented. At the same time, several resolutions adopted by \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2   as well as the de facto freeze on Turkey's EU  \par accession process have somewhat soured diplomatic relations. More recently, \par Turkey has stepped up exploratory drilling near Northern Cyprus in search of \par gas, which has drawn criticism from Cyprus as well as from the rest of the \par bloc. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   has responded with a number of measures including curbing \par high-level contact with Turkey as well as suspending \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2   \par lending for government-linked projects in Turkey. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 31 Jan 2020 16:06 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Afrms Turkey Foreign And -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Flexibility and performance profile: Unleveraged government balance sheet \par remains a key strength \par      -- We estimate that Turkey's current account posted a marginal surplus in \par 2019 for the first time in 20 years, although it should return to a deficit in \par 2020. \par      -- Although immediate balance-of-payments pressures have subsided, \par external risks remain as the Turkish economy still has to roll over external \par debt of close to 20% of GDP. \par      -- Fiscal space remains, given comparatively low net general government \par debt of under 30% of GDP. \par  \par Turkey's balance of payments has turned around substantially in the aftermath \par of the August 2018 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation. Following years of sizable deficits \par (averaging 5% of GDP in 2013-2017), the current account switched to a surplus \par in a matter of weeks in August 2018. This primarily reflects a collapse in \par imports following a sharp depreciation of the lira exchange rate. We now \par estimate that last year the current account recorded a marginal surplus for \par the first time since 2001. \par  \par Turkey's short-term balance-of-payments risks have subsided, helped by looser \par monetary policies at several developed market central banks in 2019 that \par underpinned flows of funds to emerging markets. This has provided some \par breathing room, allowing the banking system some orderly deleveraging. We have \par previously flagged the risk of Turkey's banks losing access to foreign funding \par and being unable to roll over the substantial amounts of syndicated loans \par coming due. We consider that the likelihood of this scenario materializing has \par now reduced. \par  \par Notably, however, we view balance-of-payments risks as still elevated. As a \par legacy of the past, Turkey still needs to roll over close to 20% of GDP worth \par of foreign debt every year. Tighter foreign financing conditions or domestic \par policy missteps such as an excessive growth stimulus or further sustained \par monetary loosening could present downside risks. \par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  has limited buffers to counter a potential deterioration in \par balance-of-payments, in our view. Although headline \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \par reserves amount to US$105 billion, a large proportion pertains to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \par liabilities in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the domestic banking system. This reflects \par the required reserves on banks' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) deposits, liabilities \par under the reserve option mechanism, as well as short-term swaps that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par has been increasingly using last year. Excluding the aforementioned funds, \par which may not be readily available for balance-of-payments needs, we estimate \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2 's net reserves are much smaller at US$34 billion. \par  \par Unlike its balance of payments, Turkey's fiscal position remains supportive of \par the sovereign ratings. Historically, the government ran recurrent fiscal \par deficits but these have been contained, averaging only slightly higher than 1% \par of GDP over 2013-2017. This underpins the authorities' comparatively low \par leverage today, with net general government debt at about 30% of GDP. \par  \par We estimate that last year's general government deficit widened to 3.2% of GDP \par from a 2.8% GDP deficit in 2018. This happened as lower growth negatively \par affected revenue performance, alongside the authorities implementing a fiscal \par stimulus in the first half of the year. Importantly, the government has \par implemented a series of one-off measures that have flattered the headline \par fiscal position. The most important one has been the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  paying an \par extraordinary dividend to the Treasury equivalent to 2% of GDP. The support \par package for public banks worth 0.7% of GDP, implemented in April 2019, was \par also booked below the line. Adjusted for these one-offs, the fiscal budget \par posted a deficit closer to 6% of GDP. \par  \par We anticipate that the authorities will continue to come up with ways to \par shore-up budgetary performance should it fall short of the 3% of GDP \par medium-term target. For instance, we understand that another \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  dividend, \par this time more than TRY40 billion (close to 1% of GDP), will be distributed to \par the Treasury. \par  \par Nevertheless, the government still has policy space to leverage the public \par balance sheet if needed, in our view. Such a need could arise, for example, if \par it decided to support parts of the banking system through recapitalizing \par individual institutions or undertaking a broader sector clean-up by moving \par nonperforming assets to a "bad bank." \par  \par Banks are gradually recognizing, in their books, nonperforming loans (NPLs) \par originated following the substantial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation of August 2018 and \par the consequent adjusting phase. We estimate that problematic loans (NPLs plus \par restructured loans), accounted for about 10.1% of total loans as of Sept. 30, \par 2019, and will increase over the next two years, particularly in the SME and \par construction sectors. A projected pick-up in growth should help banks work out \par accumulated NPLs and create conditions supportive of restructuring plans. In \par this context, the performance of the real estate market remains a crucial \par factor in determining the amount of losses banks will suffer. In the short \par term, we anticipate that banks' profitability--benefiting from significant \par interest rate cuts and a pick-up in lending activity--will help them absorb \par credit losses of about 250 basis points on average in 2020-2021. That said, \par capitalization remains vulnerable, particularly at state-owned banks that \par significantly expanded their loans book over the past 12 months. Positively, \par the domestic corporate sector's short \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  position has continued to decline \par throughout 2019, reaching an estimated $183 billion (24% of GDP) in August \par 2019, down from close to $220 billion (29% of GDP) in 2018. \par  \par Although it has declined by about 17% from the peak in early 2018, banks' \par reliance on short-term external funding remains significant at more than 30% \par of lending books. As such, maintaining positive investor sentiment is key for \par banks to be able to continue rolling over their short-term debt, amounting to \par about US$80.7 billion as of Nov. 30, 2019 (including nonresident deposits), \par and to fund lending expansion. \par  \par We see Turkey's monetary policy as historically ineffective in managing \par inflation. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  has never met the 5% medium-term target introduced in \par 2012, while the REER has shown substantial swings. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  has faced \par increasing political pressure in recent years, which in our view is impairing \par its effectiveness often by delaying timely responses to rising inflation. \par Inflation soared to 25% in October 2018 but has since moderated and currently \par stands at close to 11% (according to official data) in year-on-year terms. \par  \par Political pressure on the independence of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues. The president \par dismissed the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  governor in July 2019 just weeks ahead of the key decision \par on interest rates. Subsequently, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBRT\f28 \uc2 \f2  lowered the key repo rate by a \par cumulative 1,275 basis points over six months. In real terms, policy rates are \par currently close to zero. We consider that there is very limited space to cut \par interest rates further and any more monetary loosening could present risks to \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \par  \par We classify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Turkey\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime as a managed float with intermittent \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Even though outright \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales to directly \par defend the exchange rate have been limited, there have been reports of public \par banks selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the lira at difficult junctures. The regulators \par have also introduced limits on domestic banks' allowed derivative positions \par with nonresidents. The aim is to curb potential speculation against the \par exchange rate. \par  \par The long-term local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating on Turkey is one notch higher than the \par long-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating. In our view, the managed float exchange \par rate regime, comparatively developed local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capital markets, and the \par fact that about 50% of government debt is denominated in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \par almost entirely held domestically imply a lower default risk on Turkey's \par lira-denominated sovereign commercial debt than its foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated debt. This is also premised on our expectation that \par Turkey will continue to fund a large share of its financing needs in the local \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt capital markets and that the process will be transparent and \par driven by market forces. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA \par  \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For National And Regional Scale Credit \par Ratings, June 25, 2018 \par      -- Criteria | Governments | Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology, \par Dec. 18, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings, April 7, 2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks, Sept. 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments, May 18, 2009 \par  \par RELATED RESEARCH \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History, Jan. 14, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings List, Jan. 14, 2020 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators, Dec. 12, 2019. An interactive version is \par also available at " \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2 " \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2018 Annual Sovereign Default And \par Rating Transition Study, March 15, 2019 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee's assessment of the key rating factors is reflected in the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 31 Jan 2020 16:06 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Afrms Turkey Foreign And -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ratings Score Snapshot above. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par This unsolicited rating(s) was initiated by a party other than the Issuer (as \par defined in S&P Global Ratings' policies). It may be based solely on publicly \par available information and may or may not involve the participation of the \par Issuer and/or access to the Issuer's internal documents. S&P Global Ratings \par has used information from sources believed to be reliable based on standards \par established in our policies and procedures, but does not guarantee the \par accuracy, adequacy, or completeness of any information used. \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed \par to them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at www.standardandpoors.com for further \par information. A description of each of S&P Global Ratings' rating categories is \par contained in "S&P Global Ratings Definitions" at \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.standardandpoors.com/en_US/web/guest/article/-/view/sourceId/504352 }}}\uc2 \f2  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par www.capitaliq.com. All ratings affected by this rating action can be found on \par S&P Global Ratings' public website at www.standardandpoors.com. Use the \par Ratings search box located in the left column.  Alternatively, call one of the \par following S&P Global Ratings numbers: Client Support Europe (44) 20-7176-7176; \par London Press Office (44) 20-7176-3605; Paris (33) 1-4420-6708; Frankfurt (49) \par 69-33-999-225; Stockholm (46) 8-440-5914; or Moscow 7 (495) 783-4009. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , New York \par Analytical Group Contact: EMEA Sovereign and \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPF\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; \par                           SovereignIPF@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par The article with statistical information is available in full \par at www.standardandpoors.com. Registration is free. To access the full article, \par select "Ratings Actions" in the left navigation under Ratings Resources, \par then choose the Press Releases tab. \par  \par  \par  \par No content (including ratings, credit-related analyses and data, valuations, \par model, software, or other application or output therefrom) or any part thereof \par (Content) may be modified, reverse engineered, reproduced, or distributed in \par any form