\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Lira may be Turkey's Achilles heel in Syria battle\b0\par\par\uc2 1,306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2019\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story first published on Friday)\par \par \uc2 By Karin Strohecker and Tom Arnold\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Turkey's ability to sustain its military campaign in Syria may depend in part on what happens far away from the battlefield: in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The lira has long been a pinball of geopolitics and lightning rod for relations between Ankara and its Western allies, specifically Washington.\par \par \uc2 Renewed pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and attempts to stabilise it could further endanger Turkey's lean foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, leaving the country potentially with little room to defend the lira if sanctions tightened. That in turn could hurt the already fragile economy and undermine President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's ability to stand up to international pressure against his offensive in Syria.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a standoff with the United States was an aggravating factor in a 30% fall in the lira, prompting an economic recession in the country which is heavily dependent on imports as well as foreign investors flows.\par \par \uc2 By one measure, Turkey has about $36 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a number that is barely enough to defend a sustained assault on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "A shrinking pool of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, large foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rollover needs, and other economic vulnerabilities will limit Turkey's room for maneuver in Syria," said \uc2 Karl Schamotta\uc2 , director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy and structured products at Cambridge Global Payments in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 State-owned banks are reported to have helped prop up the pressured lira in recent weeks. According to one estimate, $2 billion were funnelled into markets on a single day this week to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nonetheless, it tumbled 1.5% in the initial days of the invasion nine days ago before recovering some ground.\par \par \uc2 "Net reserves are negligible in Turkey," said Tatha Ghose, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and emerging market analyst at Commerzbank in London.\par \par \uc2 "We assume that the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no real reserve resources to fight lira weakness if and when it arises," Ghose said. Even countries such as Russia and China, with their much greater reserves, have found their \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  firepower eroded quickly once pressure mounted, he added.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The deal to pause military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria has come as a respite for Turkish assets. The government has also tried to make it harder for investors to short the lira. Traders reported some Turkish banks were curbing lira supplies to overseas counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Yet, the relief is likely to be temporary. White House sanctions on a number of Turkish ministers and officials remain in place, a U.S. court case in which state lender \uc2 Halkbank\uc2  is accused of taking part in a scheme to evade U.S. sanctions on Iran continues, and a host of European countries have taken steps to limit arms sales to their \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally.\par \par \uc2 And with a U.S. congressional push for more sanctions going ahead full steam - including in a worst case scenario potential curbs on Turkey's sovereign debt - many investors fear the market euphoria may be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 The lira's history is littered with examples of tension with Washington dramatically amplifying exchange rate moves, while inflationary pressure - and its implications for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to lower interest rates - are ever present.\par \par \uc2 Yet having just emerged from a 2018 economic crisis and a near 50% collapse in the lira's exchange rate fuelled by a stand-off between Washington and Ankara over U.S. pastor \uc2 Andrew Brunson\uc2 , markets are trying to gauge just how much of a financial buffer the country has to stave off a repeat.\par \par \uc2 Foreign reserves are crucial to a country's ability to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as meet its import bills and external debt repayments.\par \par \uc2 Questions over its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves roiled markets in the spring after a report that short-term borrowed money - or swaps - had become a substantial part of Ankara's war chest.\par \par \uc2 Some investors and economists are puzzled about the best way to measure net reserves and how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts for swaps in its calculations.\par \par \uc2 While the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed net \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were slightly below $37 billion, Oxford Economics/Haver calculated that actual usable \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, taking into account beefed up gold reserves and commercial banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  requirements, stood at just $29 billion as of the end of August.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global Ratings, said on Wednesday Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were "limited", adding that "under a stress scenario, the public and private sectors may even be forced to compete for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity."\par \par \uc2 Much like in previous turmoil, economists say Ankara's focus has been on immediate damage control. The Istanbul bourse slapped a temporary short selling ban on some bank stocks, and traders reported some Turkish banks were curbing lira supplies to overseas counterparts. State lenders were reported to sell dollars to prop up the lira.\par \par \uc2 Investors expressed doubt about how effective efforts to stem a lira slide could be this time round.\par \par \uc2 "If it comes down to trying to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be successful," said Jon Harrison, managing director emerging markets macro strategy at TS Lombard.\par \par \uc2 He said while initial U.S. sanctions had been soft, the domestic pressure was ramping up on President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be tough on Turkey.\par \par \uc2 "They know they don't have the reserves to maintain the lira at a particular level and if they do they would only be able to do that for a few days."\par \par \uc2 The strength of domestic demand for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a gauge of faith in the authorities' ability to manage the lira and the country's finances. As households and businesses have shifted savings into hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollarisation has climbed to record highs, providing little reassurance.\par \par \uc2 One saving grace for Turkey is its current account balance. Following years of hefty current account deficits, last year's lira crisis tipped the country into recession - but it also closed that gap, reducing the need for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows to stabilise the exchange rate, at least in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, as the country has to import nearly all of its fuel and gas as well as lots of machinery, steel and vehicles, its reserves are also a gauge of its ability to cover its foreign buying in the event of a crisis or seizures of its overseas finances.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  estimates Turkey's reserve import cover ratio stands at about five months - above the fund's three months safe minimum though well below the around 15 months enjoyed by fellow emerging markets such as Russia, China and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's foreign debt liabilities are also under scrutiny: Over the next 12 months, some $180 billion of debt is maturing of which some $75 billion faces a higher rollover risk. The rest is made up of trade credits as well as lira and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits by non-residents, according to the Institute of International Finance.\par \par \uc2 More pressure on the lira could hurt companies' ability to pay back or roll over those dues, analysts said. Any potential sanctions restricting dollar funding for companies would be a game changer, said Nikolay Markov, senior economist at Pictet Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "If that happened, Turkish companies might face liquidity shortages, which would then require the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in," said Markov.\par \par \uc2 While it remained unclear how long Turkey's military mission in Syria will continue, it was also the potential for escalation that concerned to many investors.\par \par \uc2 "It is the speed at which things can unravel which is scary for Turkey," said Nafez Zouk at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Karin Strohecker and Tom Arnold; Additional reporting by Jonathan Spicer in Istanbul and Saqib Ahmed; Editing by Hugh Lawson and Daniel Wallis) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-10-20T11:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191020efak00fz1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
