\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXPLAINER-What is at stake in Istanbul's election re-run?\b0\par\par\uc2 986 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2019\par 07:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeat)\par \par \uc2 By Jonathan Spicer and Humeyra Pamuk\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, June 20 (Reuters) - Voters in Istanbul will head back to the polls on Sunday to choose a mayor, after Turkey's election authority scrapped the results of a March vote in which President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's ruling AK Party lost to the main opposition.\par \par \uc2 The rerun has ramifications far beyond city hall. Erdogan described the initial vote there and elsewhere in March as a "matter of survival" for Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the reasons the vote is so significant.\par \par \uc2 POWER BASE\par \par \uc2 Istanbul is Turkey's largest city and commercial hub and where Erdogan, who has dominated Turkey for 16 years, began his rise to power, serving as the city's mayor in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan's AK Party (AKP) had not lost an election there for nearly two decades before the March 31 vote, won narrowly by opposition candidate Erkrem Imamoglu before the High Election Board cancelled the result on May 6 following AKP appeals. Polls suggest the rerun will bring another tight result.\par \par \uc2 PERSONALITIES\par \par \uc2 Imamoglu, former mayor of Istanbul's Beylikduzu district, was little known across Turkey before he pulled off the upset win for the secularist Republican People's Party (CHP).\par \par \uc2 Since then, his profile has risen throughout the country and beyond as a possible chief antagonist to Erdogan, who only three months ago appeared to have an unassailable grip on power.\par \par \uc2 During days of recounts after vote, which gave Imamoglu a lead of just 13,000 in a city of 16 million, fans in football stadiums chanted for him to be given the mayoral post.\par \par \uc2 With his signature rimless eyeglasses and "Everything will be fine" slogan on billboards across Istanbul, he has avoided criticising Erdogan, focusing instead on local policy. But in the re-run campaign he has also targeted what he has called "the unjust and lawless" decision to annul the March vote.\par \par \uc2 His opponent, the AKP's \uc2 Binali Yildirim\uc2 , 63, was a former Turkish prime minister and longtime transport minister under Erdogan who led the Islamist-rooted AKP from 2016-2017 and was handpicked by the president for the mayoral race.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan campaigned hard on his behalf, holding up to eight rallies each day before the March 31 vote, most of them broadcast live on several channels, asking voters to back the AKP "as a matter of survival" for Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Yildirim initially ran a low-key campaign but has now challenged voters to stand up against "those plotting games against our country".\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 Last year Turkey's economy tipped into recession after a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis that, at its worst, halved the value of the lira and threatened to pull down the world's other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 After a partial recovery, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slid again since just before the March election and is down 11 percent against the dollar so far this year. The decision to annul the mayoral vote and schedule the June re-run prompted one of the sharpest selloffs. The weakness has also hit Turkish stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Investors have cited uncertainty over the outcome in Istanbul, as well as strained U.S.-Turkish ties, declining \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and a months-long trend of Turks snapping up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been met by a range of measures to shore up the lira.\par \par \uc2 A second CHP victory in Istanbul would transfer control over a city that accounts for a third of Turkish economic output and has a budget close to $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 DOMESTIC POLITICS\par \par \uc2 Erdogan's AK Party failed to win control of other large cities, including Ankara, in the March 31 local elections, even though the party had made an alliance with nationalists.\par \par \uc2 For Imamoglu, a second and definitive win could be the start of a fresh political career which could see him present a challenge to Erdogan.\par \par \uc2 In recent years there has been repeated media speculation that prominent AKP politicians might break away to establish a new political party. Some analysts say a defeat for the AK Party in the Istanbul rerun could speed up such preparations, although party officials deny there is any emerging split.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan had been expected to implement cabinet changes in response to the election setback, but those plans were put on hold after the re-run decision. A cabinet reshuffle may be back on the cards in case of an AKP defeat.\par \par \uc2 INTERNATIONAL RELATIONS\par \par \uc2 The result of the Istanbul mayoral election could have an indirect impact on Turkey's foreign policy at a time when Ankara is locked in a diplomatic row with its key \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally Washington and under pressure from Moscow to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in northern Syria.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been at loggerheads with the United States for months over Ankara's planned purchase of a Russian missile defense system, which Washington says is incompatible with \uc2 NATO\uc2 's defence network and could compromise its F-35 fighter jets - an aircraft Turkey is also planning to buy.\par \par \uc2 The United States is threatening to slap sanctions on Turkey unless it scraps the plan to buy the Russian S-400s. Erdogan has refused to back down and repeatedly said the missile system will be delivered in July.\par \par \uc2 While Ankara is hoping President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will waive any sanctions, an electoral defeat in Istanbul could highlight discontent among voters over Erdogan's policies and embolden any voices within his government critical of his uncompromising stance in the diplomatic row with Washington.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is also in talks with the United States over the establishment of a safe zone across its border in northeastern areas of Syria, which has been torn by civil war for eight years. Moscow, which backs Syrian President \uc2 Bashar al-Assad\uc2  whom Ankara wants gone, is pressing Turkey to rein in the rebels.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jonathan Spicer and Humeyra Pamuk; editing by Philippa Fletcher) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-6-20T06:03:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190620ef6k00id5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
