\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Four Medium-Sized Turkish Banks\b0\par\par\uc2 3,908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2019\par 20:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2019 14:05 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Four Medium-Sized Turkish Banks\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-28 February 2019: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed the Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Ratings (LTFC IDRs) of \uc2 Denizbank A.S\uc2  ., ING Bank A.S. (INGBT), QNB Finansbank A.S. and \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S \uc2 . (TEB), at 'BB-'. The Outlooks are Negative. We have also affirmed the banks' Viability Ratings (VRs) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 The support-driven LTFC IDRs of the banks' subsidiaries Deniz Finansal Kiralama A.S. (Deniz Leasing), Joint-Stock Company Denizbank Moscow (Denizbank Moscow), QNB Finans Faktoring A.S. (QNB Finans Faktoring) and QNB Finans Finansal Kiralama A.S. (QNB Finansleasing), which are equalised with those of their parents, have also been affirmed.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade of Denizbank Moscow's Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) IDR, to the level of its LTFC IDR, reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that the ability of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  to provide support, whether in the local or foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the subsidiary, is reflected in the parent's Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this commentary.\par \par \uc2 KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 - IDRS, NATIONAL RATINGS, SENIOR DEBT AND SUPPORT RATINGS OF ALL BANKS\par \par \uc2 - The banks' IDRs, National and senior debt ratings (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , TEB) are driven by potential shareholder support. This reflects \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the banks' strategic importance to their respective parents, their ownership, integration, roles within their respective groups and shared branding (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , INGBT). The Negative Outlooks on the banks' IDRs reflect those on the Turkish sovereign.\par \par \uc2 INGBT is fully owned by \uc2 ING Bank N.V\uc2  . (AA-/Stable/a+). TEB is 55% owned, but fully controlled, by TEB Holding, a holding company in which \uc2 BNP Paribas S.A\uc2  . (A+/Stable/a+) has a 50% stake. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  directly owns an additional 44.7% stake in TEB. \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  and \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  are both 99.9% owned by their respective shareholders, \uc2 Qatar National Bank\uc2  (Q.P.S.C.) (\uc2 QNB\uc2 , A+/Stable/bbb+) and \uc2 Sberbank of Russia\uc2  (BBB-/Positive/bbb-).\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  announced the potential sale of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  to UAE-based \uc2 Emirates NBD PJSC\uc2  (A+/Stable/bb+) in May 2018, although the sale has yet to be finalised. \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's base case is that \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  will continue to support \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  in case of need until the sale is finalised, given its ownership and integration.\par \par \uc2 The banks' LTFC IDRs and FC senior debt ratings are rated one notch below the Turkish sovereign LTFC IDR, reflecting our view that, in case of a marked deterioration in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector would be higher than that of a sovereign default. The Negative Outlooks on the banks' IDRs reflect those on the Turkish sovereign. The LTLC IDRs of all four banks - which are notched once below the sovereign rating - also reflect potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  continues to view the risk of capital controls being imposed in Turkey as remote given Turkey's high dependence on foreign capital (and ensuing strong incentive to retain market access) and the eventually orthodox policy response to recent market pressures. Nevertheless, in case of a marked deterioration in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that may impede the banks' ability to service their FC obligations would become more likely, in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view.\par \par \uc2 VRs\par \par \uc2 - The VRs of the four banks reflect the concentration of their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the high-risk Turkish operating environment, which deteriorated significantly in 2018. This was shown by the lira depreciation, the sharp rise in local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates (heightening pressure on the banks' margins, asset quality, capitalisation and liquidity) and weakening GDP growth. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects growth of 3.5% in 2018 and 0.6% in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The VRs also consider their moderate market shares (end-2018: between 2% and 4% of banking sector assets), limited franchises and ensuing limited competitive advantages in the volatile Turkish market. The banks provide a mix of services to corporate and commercial customers, small and medium-sized companies (SMEs, which are most sensitive to swings in the economy), and retail customers.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks for the banks have increased, as for the sector, given the weakening growth outlook, the higher Turkish lira interest rate environment and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. The banks' significant FC loan exposure and the potential impact of depreciation on often weakly hedged borrowers' ability to service their debt have added to the risks. FC lending amounted to 22% (TEB), 33% (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  ), 38% (INGBT) and 54% (\uc2 Denizbank\uc2 ) of the banks' respective gross loans at end-2018. In addition, they have significant exposure, to varying degrees, to the SME segment - which is highly sensitive to the weakening growth outlook.\par \par \uc2 Exposures to the construction (which can include real estate) and energy sectors, which have come under pressure as the operating environment has weakened, are additional sources of risk, to varying degrees at all four banks, but to a more limited extent at TEB and INGBT. INGBT has almost no electricity generation and distribution exposure. Lending to the tourism and agriculture sectors is significant at \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 , although the outlook for the tourism sector has improved.\par \par \uc2 Project finance - mainly comprising long-term, FC-denominated, slowly amortising energy and infrastructure loans - is significant at \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  and \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  (about 20% of the banks' respective gross loans). The presence of state debt assumption and revenue guarantees, and of feed-in tariffs set in US dollars in the case of some renewable energy projects, partly mitigates the credit risk.\par \par \uc2 TEB and INGBT reduced their loan books in 2018 on a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 )-adjusted basis, mainly through FC loan deleveraging, while \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  and \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  increased lending above the sector average. Growth at \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  was driven by corporate lending (including in FC) - a segment in which the bank aims to increase its market share. However, the bank reversed some of its growth in 4Q18 due to the weakening outlook. Loan growth at \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  was driven by agricultural and credit card lending but it reduced FC lending slightly in 2018.\par \par \uc2 All four banks' non-performing loan (NPL) ratios have risen. The pace of deterioration increased in 4Q18 following the heightened \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate volatility in 3Q18. NPLs amounted to 4.1% (TEB), 4.3% (INGBT), 4.6% (\uc2 Denizbank\uc2 ) and 6.1% (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  ) of the banks' respective gross loans at end-2018, versus the sector average of 3.9% (based on unconsolidated bank balance sheets).\par \par \uc2 Stage 2 loans increased at all four banks in 2018 suggesting the potential for NPL growth - albeit the rise in Stage 2 loans was partly due to the transition to IFRS 9 in 1Q18. They ranged from a moderate proportion (9% at Finansbank) to a high proportion (15%, at TEB, 17% at \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  and 24% at INGBT) of respective gross loans at end-2018. The share of restructured loans within the Stage 2 category ranged from low (INGBT) to high (\uc2 Denizbank\uc2 , \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 We expect further asset-quality weakening in 2019 given operating environment pressures, although the loan restructuring framework in Turkey could affect the migration of problematic loans to the NPL category.\par \par \uc2 The banks' capitalisation remain sensitive, as for the sector, to the lira depreciation (which inflates FC assets), albeit FC-subordinated debt from parent banks provides a partial hedge in the case of total capital ratios. Total regulatory capital ratios amounted to a high 21% (INGBT) and 17% (TEB) and a reasonable 15% (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 ) at end-2018, comfortably above the 12% recommended by the regulator.\par \par \uc2 The banks generally have fairly limited securities portfolios, with the exception of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , limiting negative revaluations of government bond portfolios in the case of interest rate rises. However, potential asset-quality deterioration remains a risk to the banks' capital positions, although pre-impairment profit provides a moderate buffer to absorb losses through income statements but is likely to weaken in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The banks' Fitch Core Capital (FCC)/risk-weighted asset (RWA) ratios ranged from a fairly low 10.0% at \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  to a moderate 11.0% at \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  and 12.0% at TEB, and a reasonable 14.1% at INGBT at end-2018.\par \par \uc2 The banks' profitability metrics remained generally reasonable in 2018. INGBT and \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  reported above-sector-average operating profit/RWA ratios (2018: 2.7% and 2.6%, respectively) underpinned by reasonable net interest margins, reflecting low funding costs (INGBT), and loan repricing and sizeable CPI-linked gains (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  ), respectively. TEB's and \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's operating profit/RWA ratios declined in 2018, albeit remaining reasonable at about 1.7% and 1.8%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects profitability to weaken in 2019 due to the weakening growth outlook, higher funding costs and higher impairment charges. It could deteriorate significantly in case of a marked weakening of asset quality.\par \par \uc2 The four banks are largely deposit funded. TEB and \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  reported below-sector-average loans/deposits ratios of 107% and 106%, respectively, at end-2018. Those of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  (130%) and INGBT (163%) were above the sector average, but have improved. In the case of INGBT, its high loans/deposits ratio partly reflects significant parent funding. \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  benefits from a stronger deposit franchise reflecting its fairly high level of pension salary accounts.\par \par \uc2 Refinancing risks have increased at the four banks, as a result of the deteriorating operating environment, recent heightened market volatility and tightening global conditions (driven by an increase in US dollar interest rates). However, with the exception of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , the banks have a lower reliance on FC wholesale funding than larger bank peers.\par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2019 14:05 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Four Medium-Sized -2-\par \par \uc2 At end-2018, net of parent funding, which is high at INGBT but moderate at the remaining banks, FC wholesale funding amounted to 7% (\uc2 Denizbank\uc2 ), 8% (TEB), 15% (INGBT) and 27% (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  ) of the banks' respective total funding. \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  is subject to \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions due to its ownership of \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and its wholesale funding primarily comprises sanction-free trade finance facilities and short-term loans.\par \par \uc2 The banks' available FC liquidity - comprising cash and interbank balances (including balances placed with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  ), maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and government securities - mean they should be able to cope with a short-lived market closure. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure in case of a prolonged loss of market access. Refinancing risks are mitigated by potential liquidity support from shareholders.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 - The affirmation of the National Ratings of all four banks and their domestic subsidiaries reflects our view that their creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 - The support-driven LTFC IDRs of Deniz Leasing, Deniz Moscow, QNB Finansleasing and QNB Finans Faktoring and, with the exception of Deniz Moscow, their LTLC IDRs and National Ratings, are equalised with those of their respective parents, \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  and \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , reflecting their strategic importance and close integration (including the sharing of risk assessment systems, customers, branding and management resources).\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 - IDRS, NATIONAL RATINGS AND SENIOR DEBT\par \par \uc2 - The banks' LT IDRs, National Ratings and Support Ratings could be downgraded if the Turkish sovereign is downgraded, or if there is a sharp reduction in the ability or propensity of a parent bank to support its Turkish subsidiary. The IDRs are also sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 VRs\par \par \uc2 - Further VR downgrades could result from a further marked deterioration in the operating environment, as reflected in negative changes in the lira exchange rate, domestic interest rates, economic growth prospects and external funding market access. A VR downgrade could result from bank-specific deterioration of asset quality, marked erosion of capital ratios, a weakening of the banks' FC liquidity positions (without this being offset by shareholder support) or deposit instability that leads to pressure on banks' liquidity and funding profiles.\par \par \uc2 The Outlooks could be revised to Stable if economic conditions stabilise and bank financial metrics do not deteriorate significantly.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 - The IDRs of the banks' subsidiaries are sensitive to a change in their parents' ratings or a change in the ability or propensity of parent banks to provide support.\par \par \uc2 The rating actions are as follows:\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  and Deniz Finansal Kiralama:\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'BB-'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'BB'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign- and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'B'\par \par \uc2 - Viability Rating (\uc2 Denizbank\uc2  only): affirmed at 'b+'\par \par \uc2 - Support Ratings: affirmed at '3'\par \par \uc2 - National Ratings: affirmed at 'AA(tur)'; Stable Outlook\par \par \uc2 Joint-Stock Company Denizbank Moscow:\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR: affirmed at 'BB-'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR: downgraded to 'BB-' from 'BB'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign- and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'B'\par \par \uc2 - Support Ratings: affirmed at '3'\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , QNB Finans Finansal Kiralama A.S., QNB Finans Faktoring A.S.:\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'BB-'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'BB'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign- and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'B'\par \par \uc2 - Viability Rating (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  only): affirmed at 'b+'\par \par \uc2 - Support Ratings: affirmed at '3'\par \par \uc2 - National Ratings: affirmed at 'AA(tur)'; Stable Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Senior unsecured long-term debt (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  only): affirmed at 'BB-'\par \par \uc2 - Senior unsecured short-term debt (\uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  only): affirmed at 'B'\par \par \uc2 ING Bank A.S.:\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR: affirmed at 'BB-'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR: affirmed at 'BB'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign- and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs: affirmed at 'B'\par \par \uc2 - Viability Rating: affirmed at 'b+'\par \par \uc2 - Support Rating: affirmed at '3'\par \par \uc2 - National Rating: affirmed at 'AA(tur)'; Stable Outlook\par \par \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR: affirmed at 'BB-'; Negative Outlook\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight