\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID EUROPE-Turkey's crisis is starting to spread\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 09:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (A look at the day ahead from EMEA markets editor Mike Dolan. The views expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Aug 13 (Reuters) - As Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s lira crisis deepens, contagion across all emerging markets has widened \u8211\'20\'13 dragging in the likes of South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s rand, Argentina and Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s pesos, Russia\u8217\'20\'19s rouble and emerging market stock markets around the globe. MSCI\u8217\'20\'19s benchmark index of global emerging stock markets fell to near its 2018 lows, with many emerging Asia exchanges getting hammered. Indonesian stocks, for example, fell more than 3 percent, with South Korean and Turkish stocks down more than 1 percent. The ripple effects more generally were felt in bids for "safety" plays -- U.S. Treasuries, German Bunds, Japan\u8217\'20\'19s yen and the dollar more broadly. Curiously, neither gold nor Swiss francs has been boosted.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yen was undermined in part as Japanese household savers have typically held large amounts of high-yielding emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets and pressure for redemptions and repatriation may now rise and be indiscriminate across the EM complex. Contagion is also fueled by concern that fund managers who have lost heavily in Turkey or other emerging assets will look to sell more liquid parts of those portfolios to meet expected redemptions.\par \par \uc2 The other area of contagion is via western banks and creditors, who have direct exposure to Turkey and the worst affected markets and who will look to tighten credit or scale back \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  there. With several European banks, including \uc2 BBVA\uc2 , \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 , ING and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , owning Turkish bank stakes, the focus has been on the fallout in the euro banking sector and euro/dollar has sunk to its lowest in over a year as a result, dropping as low as $1.1363 early on Monday before recovering to above $1.14.\par \par \uc2 Wider European or U.S. economic or financial contagion beyond direct Turkish bank holdings is considered relatively limited, however. European and U.S. stock futures were down about half a percent first thing Monday.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/lira soared to a record above 7.2 early on Monday before rebounding in wildly volatile trading. Turkish officials flagged another set piece proposal to tackle the crisis later on Monday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps activity while loosening reserve requirements to provide extra liquidity to its ailing banks. Some local banks suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  services for retail customers. But there was no sign so far of the hefty interest rate rises of 5 percent points or more that many market participants say will now be required to address the loss of confidence in the lira. And there is widespread concern about some form of capital controls being put in place as \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  has neither adequate foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buoy the lira or support its banks and is reluctant to seek conditional help from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , as would be typical in this sort of crisis.\par \par \uc2 With the lira now having lost 43 percent of its value so far in 2018, its weakening to levels above 7 per dollar is highly sensitive. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimated last week that Turkish banks\u8217\'20\'19 excess capital would be wiped out at roughly 7.1 per dollar, for example. While Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s economic policy management has been in question to date, the crisis has been exaggerated by a diplomatic and trade row with the United States, and President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s doubling of steel tariffs on Turkey on Friday added massive pressure to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There was little sign of any resolution to that row over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 * Europe corp events: \uc2 1&1 Drillisch\uc2 , Flughafen Zurich (Traffic), \uc2 Fraport AG\uc2  (Traffic), Scout24, \uc2 United Internet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Turkey's government to detail crisis measures to support lira\par \par \uc2 * Italy government bond auction; July inflation data\par \par \uc2 * Czech June current account\par \par \uc2 * Israel July trade\par \par \uc2 * Sweden July jobless\par \par \uc2 * US Q2 earnings: \uc2 Sysco\uc2  (Reporting by Mike Dolan; editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-13T08:40:17.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180813ee8d00x9a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
