\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Turkey says has action plan to ease market concerns; lira firms from record low\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2018\par 23:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Finance Minister Albayrak says Turkey drafted action plan\par \par \uc2 * Turkey taking measures on Monday morning to ease concerns\par \par \uc2 * Albayrak says no plans to seize or convert dollar accounts\par \par \uc2 * Erdogan says lira weakness an economic war against Turkey (Updates with lira firming, banking watchdog move)\par \par \uc2 By Humeyra Pamuk\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, Aug 12 (Reuters) - Turkey has drafted a economic action plan and will start implementing it on Monday morning to ease investor concerns, Finance Minister Berat Albayrak said on Sunday, after the lira plunged to a new record low in early Asia Pacific trade.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Hurriyet newspaper published online, Albayrak described the lira's weakness as "an attack," echoing President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  - who is his father-in-law - and said the action plan was ready.\par \par \uc2 "From Monday morning onwards our institutions will take the necessary steps and will share the announcements with the market," Albayrak said, without giving details on what the steps would be.\par \par \uc2 Albayrak also said a plan has been prepared for banks and the real economy sector, including small to mid-sized businesses which are most affected by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations. "We will be taking the necessary steps with our banks and banking watchdog in a speedy manner," he said.\par \par \uc2 He has also dismissed any suggestions that \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar-denominated bank accounts, saying any seizure or conversion of those deposits into lira was out of the question.\par \par \uc2 The lira plunged to a fresh record low of 7.24 against the dollar during in Asia Pacific trade, where markets were opening for Monday morning. It pared losses after Albayrak's comments and stood at 6.8603 at 2136 GMT Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost more than 45 percent of its value this year, largely over worries about Erdogan's influence over the economy, his repeated calls for lower interest rates in the face of high inflation and worsening ties with the United States.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the lira tumbled as much as 18 percent at one point, its biggest daily drop since 2001.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Erdogan had stood by his opposition to high interest rates, saying they were an instrument of exploitation and that Turkey was not going to fall into this trap.\par \par \uc2 In the Hurriyet interview, Albayrak said budget policies were important to support and strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policies. "We will be entering a strong period in terms of fiscal policies," he said.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's banking watchdog BBDK in a statement said it was limiting banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap transactions.\par \par \uc2 STORM IN A TEA CUP\par \par \uc2 Erdogan, who has called himself the "enemy of interest rates," wants cheap credit from banks to fuel growth, but investors fear the economy is overheating and could be set for a hard landing.\par \par \uc2 His comments on interest rates - and his recent appointment of his son-in-law as finance minister - have heightened perceptions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not independent.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, speaking to supporters in Trabzon on the Black Sea coast, Erdogan dismissed suggestions that Turkey was in a financial crisis like those seen in Asia two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 The lira's free-fall was the result of a plot and did not reflect Turkey's economic fundamentals, he said. "What is the reason for all this storm in a tea cup? There is no economic reason... This is called carrying out an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Turkey," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates to support the lira in an emergency move in May, but it did not tighten monetary policy at its last meeting.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan repeated his call for Turks to sell dollars and buy lira to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while telling business owners not to stock up on dollars.\par \par \uc2 "I am specifically addressing our manufacturers: Do not rush to the banks to buy dollars. Do not take a stance saying 'We are bankrupt, we are done, we should guarantee ourselves.' If you do that, that would be wrong. You should know that to keep this nation standing is ... also the manufacturers' duty."\par \par \uc2 DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 Turkey's row with the United States has further weighed on the lira.\par \par \uc2 The two \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies have been at odds over a wide range of issues: diverging interests in Syria, Ankara's ambition to buy Russian defence systems and more recently the case of \uc2 Andrew Brunson\uc2 , an evangelical pastor on trial in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 After Brunson had been in jail for almost 20 months, a court in July ordered him to be moved to house arrest. Since then U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  have called for his release, while Ankara says the decision is up to the courts.\par \par \uc2 Washington in response sanctioned two Turkish ministers, and Trump on Friday announced the United States was doubling tariffs on steel and aluminium imports from Turkey, saying relations with Ankara were "not good at this time."\par \par \uc2 Last week, a Turkish delegation went to Washington and met U.S. counterparts but there was no breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Erdogan revealed details of talks between the two countries over the pastor, saying the United States had given Turkey until last Wednesday to hand him over. "They threatened to sanction our ministers if we didn't," he said.\par \par \uc2 A White House official said they did not set a deadline for Brunson, adding that he was expected home a long time ago. The official said diplomacy on the issue continues. (Reporting by Humeyra Pamuk Additional reporting by Ezgi Erkoyun and Jeff Mason in Bridgewater, New Jersey Editing by David Stamp and Cynthia Osterman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-12T22:52:28.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180812ee8c00r8l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
