\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Why does the Turkish lira plummet and turn on the warning lights in Europe?\b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK - Concerns about the fragile Turkish economy and the risk of contagion in Europe made investors nervous on Friday and sent the lira to a record low against the US dollar. The Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  collapsed up to 17% against the dollar, reflecting a number of concerns, including tensions with the United States and the unwillingness of the Turkish authorities to raise interest rates. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who imposed sanctions on senior Turkish officials earlier this month for his role in the arrest of a US pastor, raised bets on Friday with the promise of raising metal rates to Turkey. Turkish President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  was defiant. "Do not forget this: if you have dollars, we have our people, justice and God," he said. "We will come out of the economic war successfully." Rhetoric did little to calm the markets. The lira, which has fallen almost 40% against the dollar this year, resumed its fall while Erdogan spoke.\par \par \uc2  According to Banco BASE, it is not ruled out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the objective of avoiding a further fall of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Rodrigo Catril\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney, said that investors are increasingly worried by the increase in inflation and the capacity of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose independence has been questioned by investors, to do something about it. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under pressure from Erdogan, who was re-elected in June, to keep interest rates low despite inflation exceeding 15% in July. It goes against market expectations and left the policy unchanged at its most recent meeting. That may have pleased Erdogan, but economists say it is now likely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to take emergency measures. "There are reasons to believe that the rise in emergency interest rates during the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis could provide ephemeral relief," said \uc2 William Jackson\uc2 , chief economist for emerging markets at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . "It is not clear if Turkey can go back from the edge this time," he added. The government has already reduced its growth forecast for this year to 4% from 5.5%, but economists warn that the fall will be much worse if confidence is not restored quickly. "You can not rule out a recession and a debt crisis that would force Turkey to implement capital controls and ask for a bailout [of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 ]," said Carsten Hesse, Berenberg's European economist. Erdogan seems determined to fight. This Friday, he urged the Turks to exchange dollars and euros for liras to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, other forces were working against the lira. Trump said in a tweet on Friday that he would raise taxes on Turkish metal. "I just authorized a doubling of tariffs on steel and aluminum with respect to Turkey because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Turkish lira, is rapidly sliding down against our strong dollar, aluminum will now have 20% and steel 50%. Turkey are not good at the moment! ", Reads the tweet.Tweet It was not immediately clear when the rate increases would be imposed. It is not just Turkey's problem Investor concerns have turned in recent days to the health of Turkish banks. The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reported that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned about the exposure of eurozone banks to Turkey due to the lira. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  refused to comment. Data from the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  show that eurozone banks have loans worth more than 150,000 million in Turkey. The Spanish, French and Italian banks are the most exposed. The actions of some of the largest banks inEurope were hit hard on Friday. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  of Italy lost 5.6% and Spanish lender \uc2 BBVA\uc2  fell 5.5%. The French BNP Paribas lost 4.3% and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  fell 5.3%. The euro traded 0.9% less against the dollar on Friday. The Turkish economy has expanded rapidly this year compared to 2017. But its growth in recent years has been driven by construction financed largely by foreign investors. Investors worry about the country's ability to attract money in difficult times to settle their debts. "The decrease in the Turkish lira and the increase in borrowing costs cause a big headache for many Turkish companies, since they have borrowed in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite receiving local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  income," said Hesse. - Gul Tuysuz and Chris Liakos contributed to this report.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180810ee8a0049e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
