\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-\uc2 Turkey  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says to return to single rate, lira rallies\b0\par\par\uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2018\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Nevzat Devranoglu\par \par \uc2 ANKARA, May 28 (Reuters) - \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would return to using the one-week repo rate as its benchmark, the latest move by authorities to reassure investors about the direction of monetary policy and give the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost.\par \par \uc2 At an emergency meeting last week the bank hiked its late liquidity window rate, the highest of the four interest rates it uses, by 3 percentage points to 16.5 percent. That followed a 20 percent tumble in the lira this year on deepening concerns about President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's grip on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 For years the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has relied on multiple rates to set borrowing costs, creating a complex system that economists say has made policy less predictable. It has funded the financial system through the late liquidity window for months, all while keeping the repo rate steady at 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unwillingness to use the repo rate has exacerbated concerns that it is less than independent. Erdogan, a self-described "enemy of interest rates" has repeatedly called for lower borrowing costs to fuel credit and construction.\par \par \uc2 The bank said in a statement the repo rate would be set at 16.5 percent and would once again be regarded as the main rate. The new framework would take effect on June 1, it said.\par \par \uc2 "We think conducting funding through a single rate, the weekly repo rate, has increased predictability and sends a powerful signal to the markets that the tight stance will continue," Is Investment economist Muammer Komurcuoglu said.\par \par \uc2 The lira firmed 2.4 percent to 4.5950 against the U.S. dollar, putting it on track for its best one-day performance in 16 months. It remains one of the worst performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and touched a record low of 4.9290 last week.\par \par \uc2 'SIMPLIFICATION'\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday's move had completed its longstanding drive for "simplification" -- the term it uses for the need to move to a single rate. The overnight borrowing and lending rates will be determined at 150 basis points below and above the one-week repo rate, it said.\par \par \uc2 The late liquidity window rate will be 300 basis points above the repo, it said.\par \par \uc2 Monday's decision complemented the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate hike last week, and was a positive, said Sebastien Barbe, global head of emerging markets research and strategy at Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 "It is opening the door to another tightening, another hike in interest rates in the coming weeks if there is a need to do it," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Eventually they can eliminate all other rates and go with one rate but in the short term I would expect them to keep this kind of system ... because it gives them flexibility."\par \par \uc2 The lira sell-off accelerated this month after Erdogan said he would look to take greater control of monetary policy following June 24 presidential and parliamentary elections. That deepened alarm among investors already disconcerted by Erdogan's growing authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision came after days of meetings among \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top economic ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the prime minister to convince Erdogan of the need for a sharp increase in interest rates, Reuters reported last week.\par \par \uc2 Authorities have since been on a push to reassure investors.\par \par \uc2 Murat Cetinkaya, the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Mehmet Simsek\uc2  are due to meet with foreign investors in London on Monday and Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 They held a similar meeting with investors in Turkey on Sunday, at which Cetinkaya flagged the return to a more orthodox policy framework, according to one banker who was present.\par \par \uc2 Bankers said Simsek's comments focused on the government's achievements and plans, stressing fiscal discipline, tight monetary policy and its commitment to the market economy.\par \par \uc2 Simsek said in similar comments last week that Turkey would not try to push back against financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "We understand the concerns of the market. We understand the worries that investors might have," he told broadcaster NTV. "We took the necessary steps to address these concerns and we will continue to do so."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ezgi Erkoyun Writing by Daren Butler and David Dolan; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-28T14:45:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180528ee5s005vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
