\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Turkish lira volatile,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  moves to help firms on foreign debt\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2018\par 19:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Fitch, graphic, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Behiye Selin Taner and Nevzat Devranoglu\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, May 25 (Reuters) - Turkey's lira wobbled in volatile trade on Friday, as investors tried to gauge prospects of another interest rates increase after this week's emergency action by authorities struggling to stabilise the plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which raised its top interest rate by 3 percentage points to 16.5 percent on Wednesday, followed up on Friday with a move to help companies repay some foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen about 20 percent this year, hit by investors' concern about the strengthening influence of President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  on monetary policy. Ratings agency Fitch warned that the sharp decline could put pressure on the many Turkish firms that borrow money in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lira, which hit a record low of 4.9290 just before Wednesday's rate rise, was trading at 4.7049 to the dollar at 1654 GMT on Friday, little changed from the previous day's close.\par \par \uc2 However, it had weakened as much as 2 percent in early trade before recovering, helped partly by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which said it would allow some foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt to be repaid at fixed lira rates.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan, a self-proclaimed "enemy of interest rates" who faces elections next month, wants lower borrowing costs to fuel credit growth and economic expansion.\par \par \uc2 Investors fear the economy is overheating and want substantial rate increases to tame inflation. They were particularly unnerved by comments from Erdogan last week that he would look to take greater control over monetary policy after the elections.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Mehmet Simsek\uc2 , a former Wall Street banker, tried on Friday to soothe such worries.\par \par \uc2 "We understand the worries that investors might have. We took the necessary steps to address these concerns and we will continue to do so," he told broadcaster NTV. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that it is independent and it would do whatever is necessary when it is necessary."\par \par \uc2 Simsek and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Murat Cetinkaya are due to meet investors in Turkey on Sunday and in London next week, bankers said.\par \par \uc2 RAPID GROWTH\par \par \uc2 "Turkish officials essentially are doing everything they can to restore the confidence that was seriously undermined by allowing the lira to fall perhaps for too long," said \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2 , an emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 The lira has had its worst week and month in almost nine years. Markets are sensing further weakness ahead and could push authorities to do more to turn the tide. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to have its next policy-setting meeting on June 7.\par \par \uc2 Signals from Turkey's interest rate markets are tricky to decipher but they suggest there will be at least one more rate rise, although it may be smaller than this week's move.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan has ruled for 15 years, overseeing a period of rapid economic growth. But a widespread crackdown against his opponents has led rights groups and \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to voice concerns about growing authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 It took days of preparation by \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top economic ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the prime minister to persuade Erdogan of the need for a sharp increase in interest rates, three people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 While Erdogan made some effort to placate investors after Wednesday's rate rise - talking about the need for fiscal discipline and stability - he also said the lira's volatility did not reflect economic realities and he would not allow "global governance types" to ruin the country.\par \par \uc2 Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move, designed to ease the debt burden on companies, affects about $3.5 billion of loan repayments, bankers said.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Turkey's benchmark 10-year bond was flat on Friday. The main BIST 100 share index rose 2 percent to 103,179.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ali Kucukgocmen, Ezgi Erkoyun, Karin Strohecker and Marc Jones Writing by Daren Butler and David Dolan; Editing by Catherine Evans, John Stonestreet and David Stamp) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-25T18:38:50.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180525ee5p0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
