\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-After "late" rate hike, Turkey won't oppose markets, Simsek says\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2018\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds quotes and context)\par \par \uc2 By Ezgi Erkoyun and Ali Kucukgocmen\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL, May 25 (Reuters) - Turkey won't look to push back against financial markets, Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Mehmet Simsek\uc2  said on Friday, although he acknowledged investors remained concerned after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slow to react with a rate increase this week.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday hiked its top interest rate by 3 percentage points to 16.5 percent at an emergency meeting called to avert a full-blown crisis in the lira. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tumbled some 20 percent this year on deepening concern about President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's grip on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey will solve all its issues with a rules-based, market economy and will not stand stubbornly against markets," Simsek said in an interview with broadcaster NTV.\par \par \uc2 "We understand the concerns of the market. We understand the worries that investors might have. We took the necessary steps to address these concerns and we will continue to do so."\par \par \uc2 Simsek, a former Wall Street banker, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Murat Cetinkaya are due to meet with overseas investors next week, in what are likely to be talks aimed at soothing concerns about the outlook for Turkish policy.\par \par \uc2 They will also meet with representatives of Turkey's financial and business world in Istanbul on Sunday, bankers said.\par \par \uc2 Investors appear to remain skeptical. The lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially recovered after Wednesday's emergency rate hike but has since given up a large slice of those gains.\par \par \uc2 Simsek acknowledged that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been slow to react, although he said it had been effective.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a strong step although it was a late one, and it was effective," he said.\par \par \uc2 It took days of preparation by \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top economic ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the prime minister to convince President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  of the need for a sharp increase in interest rates, three people familiar with the matter told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 A self-described "enemy of interest rates" Erdogan has, with elections just a month away, repeatedly called for lower borrowing costs to fuel credit growth and spending.\par \par \uc2 International investors want to see higher rates to rein in double-digit inflation and have dumped the lira on concerns about Erdogan's influence over monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Simsek said the emergency rate hike proved the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence and that it would continue to take necessary measures with the support of the government. (Additional reporting by Nevzat Devranoglu in Ankara Writing by David Dolan; Editing by Daren Butler and Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-25T14:45:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180525ee5p00cie\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
