\par\par \b \uc2 Is The Emerging Market Rally Over? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dimitra DeFotis \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2016\par 18:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 It was another week of record emerging-market bond funds, and the iShares MSCI Emerging Market exchange-traded fund ( EEM) is up 12% year to date.\par \par \uc2 Equities in individual countries have not followed in sync, including shares in Greece, Egypt and Poland. The \uc2 Global X MSCI Greece ETF\uc2  (GREK) is down 1% today, while the iShares MSCI Turkey ETF (TUR) is up nearly 4%, undoing some of the coup-attempt selloff. The Turkey ETF is now flat for the year. The Turkish lira (TRY) was stronger by 1.3% against the U.S. dollar in recent trading, bringing its decline against the greenback to 3.6% year to date.\par \par \uc2 The iShares JP Morgan USD Emerging Markets Bond ETF (EMB) is up nearly 10% this year. Strategist Bob Savage at Track Research in New York asks what's next for emerging markets in his summary of last week's global markets. Two excerpts:\par \par \uc2 "... Is the EM rally over? For equities maybe, for bonds not yet. The MSCI EM equity index fell 0.1% this week -- mostly because of Turkey but there are other reasons and they matter with the ...U.S. was about to seize $1 billion in assets and adding to Malaysian ringgit (MYR) pressures. With the Nigerian naira moving over 300 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm while black market trades to 375. \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  equity fallout matter to the region with the Erdogan declaration of a state of emergency for 3 months seen as a way of continuing the clearing out of any opposition to his regime, while the \uc2 EU\uc2  and US continue to warn against this. When it comes to rates, Indonesia remained on hold and the IDR gains while the Brazil COPOM stayed on hold and worried markets as the inflation outlook remains mixed into the Olympics -- with the terror arrests in Rio another factor for Brazil in the weeks ahead. The key issue for EM Is bonds which continued to attract foreigners -- EM flows show a new record high for bonds up $4.92 billion on the week after $2.69 billion last week. The key issue is the FOMC and whether the statement this week ahead drives a risk reduction on lift-off fears. The SF Fed Economic Letter is worth considering in this debate ..."\par \par \uc2 Of the G-20 meetings in China, Savage said they "highlight the need for coordinated responses to global deflationary risks, on going doubts about the role of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in economic adjustments from Chinese yuan (CNY) to Japanese yen (JPY) to Great British pound (GBP) and as the world grapples with terrorism." He sums up the data through July 22 in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 "U.S. dollar index rose 0.7% to 97.35 on the week with Turkey again losing for EM off 2.3% to 3.0590 while the New Zealand dollar (NZD) continued to fall off 1.75% to .7000 in G10. But the real story in EM was one of contrasts with Russian ruble (RUB) off 2.2% to 64.62 while South Africa rand (ZAR) continued to gain 2% to 14.3585 on the week. Even as the EUR lost a bit of ground last week, Polish zloty (PLN) gained up 0.6% to 3.9690. Brazil also continued to rally up 0.7% to 3.2570 with terror arrests ahead of the Olympics and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on hold but MXN again lost ground to 18.60 off 0.5%. In Asia, the CNY surprised with gains after what seemed like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 6.70 ahead of the G20 meetings. Indian rupee (INR) lost ground on further RBI replacement doubts, bonds up and stock flows weaker -- off 0.1% to 67.154. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  