\par\par \b \uc2 Universal Health Services Investors Want More Say in Board -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2016\par 00:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 22:15 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Influential investors are agitating for more say over who sits on the board of hospital operator Universal Health Services. The \uc2 California Public Employees' Retirement System\uc2  and the New York City Pension Funds, in a letter to shareholders, threw support behind a proposal to give investors with at least 3% ownership the ability to nominate directors. A growing number of companies have adopted similar proposals, the shareholders said. \uc2 UHS\uc2  has urged shareholders to reject the proposal, which could "introduce an unnecessary and potentially expensive and destabilizing dynamic" into board elections, the company said. Its annual meeting is scheduled for May 18. (melanie.evans@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 18:07 ET - Loans and leases for business equipment last month dropped 11% from a year earlier to $8.1B, says a survey of 25 major credit providers by the \uc2 Equipment Leasing and Finance Association\uc2 . 1Q financing volume fell 8.5% from last year as the pull back underway in capex gained momentum. "Businesses are uncertain about the outlook for strong and consistent growth in the U.S. economy," says \uc2 Ralph Petta\uc2 , president of the association. Loan and lease payments past due by 30 days receded to 1.2% of survey respondents' net receivables from 1.4% in February, but still above 1.18% in March 2015. (robert.tita@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 18:03 EDT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Before returning to her home in southern Argentina this week, former president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  met with dozens of mayors at her new Fatherland Institute in Argentina's capital. She called for the creation of a new "Citizens Front" to block President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  and his economic initiatives. But the call for the new Front underscores how the influence of her Victory Front party has waned since she left office in December. A new Front wouldn't be necessary if the old one were effectively opposing Macri. The Victory Front is fractured and shows no signs of making a comeback anytime soon. Earlier this year, a dozen of its key members left, saying they thought conversing with Macri wasn't a bad idea. Later, other members voted for Macri's plan to settle up with holdout bondholders, something Kirchner had vowed never to do. (taos.turner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:58 ET - For the first time \uc2 Novartis\uc2  (NOVN.EB) issued 2016 sales guidance for new heart-failure drug Entresto, and it came in about $100M below consensus, Bernstein says. The bank and other analysts had been forecasting $300M-$400M in sales this year. Some health insurers have been hampering uptake of the drug by requiring doctors to get prior authorization from the insurer before prescribing it, Bernstein says. Also, doctors don't feel a "sense of urgency" to prescribe Entresto if their patients are stable on other treatments, the bank adds. Entresto had sales of $17M in 1Q. Novartis Chief Executive \uc2 Joe Jimenez\uc2  says the company increased its US sales force for Entresto by 50% to make more frequent visits to cardiologists. (Jeanne.whalen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:51 ET - Argentina's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged to $35.8B after the country sold $16.5B in new bonds, the largest ever sale by any emerging market country or company. Reserves rose $6.6B from the previous day, Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. Most of the rest of the money from the bond sale went to pay bondholders that had sued Argentina in the US. The increase in reserves offers relief to a country whose hard cash holdings had fallen from more than $52B in 2011, when then president \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  cracked down on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading and made it illegal for most people and companies to buy US dollars. The move aimed at bolstering the reserves, but it backfired, leading to increased black-market demand for dollars. President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  ended the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in December. (taos.turner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:42 ET - In a season of emerging-market turmoil, Turkey's economy is proving it's more resilient than some expected. But that's not to say it won't face troubles ahead. "Given large financing needs and a high share of short-term capital inflows, Turkey remains vulnerable to capital flow reversal," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says. Despite large corporate exposure to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily and allow the lira to depreciate. The lira, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says, is still 5-10% overvalued. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are too low it adds, warning the country to boost its rainy-day stockpile. "Reserve accumulation is warranted" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to smoothing periods of excessive volatility," says the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . (ian.talley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:36 ET - Net bets on a lower copper price outnumbered those on copper, rising by 8,164 contracts in the week to Tuesday, the second week in a row of bearish sentiment for the metal. The number of net bearish bets stood at 9,688 in the previous week. Prices have risen since Tuesday, however, lifted by a bump in oil prices. Copper closed up 0.6% at $2.2645 a pound. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:29 ET - \uc2 Office Depot\uc2  (ODP) CEO \uc2 Roland Smith\uc2 's compensation slid to $4.3M last year, down from $5.1M a year earlier mostly because of a smaller bonus, securities filings show. Smith's compensation for the last two years is almost entirely cash rather that the stock-based payouts that tend to pump executive pay up to eight figures. The ODP boss is still teed up for such a payout if \uc2 Staples\uc2  (SPLS) buys the company--as much as $23.4M worth. But that's a big "if" as a federal judge considers whether to block the deal on antitrust grounds. (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald)\par \par \uc2 17:24 ET - Net bullish bets on gold by hedge funds and other speculative investors increased in the week to Tuesday. Bets on gold's rise outnumbered those on a lower gold price by 188,030 contracts, data from the CFTC showed, the biggest margin since October 2012. Bets stood at 184,218 contracts in the previous period. The increase in gold longs has alarmed some investors, who believe the market is vulnerable to any signs of hawkishness from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which will conclude its monetary policy meeting Wednesday. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:21 ET - Hedge funds, pension funds and other money managers moved into bullish crude positions and out of bearish crude positions during the week ended Tuesday, according to CFTC data. These fund managers added 14,446 bets on higher prices and cut 15,910 bets on bearish crude prices. That expanded their net-long position by 14% to 245,987, its strongest position since mid-May. Prices lost 2.6% in the reporting week after the world's biggest exporters failed to negotiate a cap on their output at a widely-watched meeting during the weekend. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:17 ET - Speculative bets on the Japanese yen rose to $8.2B in the week ended Tuesday, a fresh record according to Scotiabank and CFTC data. That leaves investors "exceptionally vulnerable" to swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of next week's \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  policy meeting. Some investors could have already been caught wrong-footed as the yen weakened 2% against the dollar amid speculation that the BoJ was considering expanding its negative-rates policy. Meanwhile, bearish dollar bets on the US dollar hit $1.8B in the week ended Tuesday. Hedge funds and other money managers haven't been this bearish on the dollar since mid-2014. That likely also caught investors off guard as the buck rebounded this week on hopes for US interest-rate increases. (Chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:56 ET - Ukraine's new government may have resolved some of the uncertainty hanging over the country, but creditors are maintaining a healthy does of wariness, especially after handful of the most reform-minded officials left in the reshuffle. The US, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and Europe want Kiev to show they're committed to economic overhauls and anticorruption efforts before they allow any more financing to move ahead. In a call with new PM Volodymyr Groysman and new Finance Minister Oleksandr Danylyuk, US Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2 , "emphasized the importance of the new government adhering to the reform agenda and completing the current \uc2 IMF\uc2  review, as soon as possible in order to restart the flow of international assistance," \uc2 Treasury\uc2  says. "Secretary Lew stressed the importance of further progress on fighting corruption, including putting in place a new, reformist Prosecutor General." Kiev needs that cash to help keep the country afloat. (ian.talley@wsj.com;@iantalley)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2016 18:17 ET (22:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160422ec4m004q0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
