\par\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2 : Turkish Lira 5%-10% Overvalued,  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Reserves Too Low -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2016\par 23:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:42 ET - In a season of emerging-market turmoil, Turkey's economy is proving it's more resilient than some expected. But that's not to say it won't face troubles ahead. "Given large financing needs and a high share of short-term capital inflows, Turkey remains vulnerable to capital flow reversal," the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says. Despite large corporate exposure to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily and allow the lira to depreciate. The lira, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says, is still 5-10% overvalued. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are too low it adds, warning the country to boost its rainy-day stockpile. "Reserve accumulation is warranted" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to smoothing periods of excessive volatility," says the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . (ian.talley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:36 ET - Net bets on a lower copper price outnumbered those on copper, rising by 8,164 contracts in the week to Tuesday, the second week in a row of bearish sentiment for the metal. The number of net bearish bets stood at 9,688 in the previous week. Prices have risen since Tuesday, however, lifted by a bump in oil prices. Copper closed up 0.6% at $2.2645 a pound. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:29 ET - \uc2 Office Depot\uc2  (ODP) CEO \uc2 Roland Smith\uc2 's compensation slid to $4.3M last year, down from $5.1M a year earlier mostly because of a smaller bonus, securities filings show. Smith's compensation for the last two years is almost entirely cash rather that the stock-based payouts that tend to pump executive pay up to eight figures. The ODP boss is still teed up for such a payout if \uc2 Staples\uc2  (SPLS) buys the company--as much as $23.4M worth. But that's a big "if" as a federal judge considers whether to block the deal on antitrust grounds. (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald)\par \par \uc2 17:24 ET - Net bullish bets on gold by hedge funds and other speculative investors increased in the week to Tuesday. Bets on gold's rise outnumbered those on a lower gold price by 188,030 contracts, data from the CFTC showed, the biggest margin since October 2012. Bets stood at 184,218 contracts in the previous period. The increase in gold longs has alarmed some investors, who believe the market is vulnerable to any signs of hawkishness from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which will conclude its monetary policy meeting Wednesday. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:21 ET - Hedge funds, pension funds and other money managers moved into bullish crude positions and out of bearish crude positions during the week ended Tuesday, according to CFTC data. These fund managers added 14,446 bets on higher prices and cut 15,910 bets on bearish crude prices. That expanded their net-long position by 14% to 245,987, its strongest position since mid-May. Prices lost 2.6% in the reporting week after the world's biggest exporters failed to negotiate a cap on their output at a widely-watched meeting during the weekend. (tim.puko@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:17 ET - Speculative bets on the Japanese yen rose to $8.2B in the week ended Tuesday, a fresh record according to Scotiabank and CFTC data. That leaves investors "exceptionally vulnerable" to swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of next week's \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  policy meeting. Some investors could have already been caught wrong-footed as the yen weakened 2% against the dollar amid speculation that the BoJ was considering expanding its negative-rates policy. Meanwhile, bearish dollar bets on the US dollar hit $1.8B in the week ended Tuesday. Hedge funds and other money managers haven't been this bearish on the dollar since mid-2014. That likely also caught investors off guard as the buck rebounded this week on hopes for US interest-rate increases. (Chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:56 ET - Ukraine's new government may have resolved some of the uncertainty hanging over the country, but creditors are maintaining a healthy does of wariness, especially after handful of the most reform-minded officials left in the reshuffle. The US, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and Europe want Kiev to show they're committed to economic overhauls and anticorruption efforts before they allow any more financing to move ahead. In a call with new PM Volodymyr Groysman and new Finance Minister Oleksandr Danylyuk, US Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2 , "emphasized the importance of the new government adhering to the reform agenda and completing the current \uc2 IMF\uc2  review, as soon as possible in order to restart the flow of international assistance," \uc2 Treasury\uc2  says. "Secretary Lew stressed the importance of further progress on fighting corruption, including putting in place a new, reformist Prosecutor General." Kiev needs that cash to help keep the country afloat. (ian.talley@wsj.com;@iantalley)\par \par \uc2 16:37 ET - Chemical & Mining Co. of Chile, or SQM, notified the US and Chile of results from a foreign corruption probe and is cooperating with authorities, the company says in a securities filing. The chemical company disclosed in 2015 it was carrying out an investigation over possible Foreign Corrupt Practices Act violations regarding "certain payments made by SQM between the tax years 2009 and 2015...that may not have been properly supported or that may not have been necessary to generate corporate income. The company had reported in December that its review, conducted in part by law firm \uc2 Shearman & Sterling\uc2 , found evidence of payments made without significant documentation but there was no evidence identified that demonstrated payments were made to influence a public official. (stephen.dockery@wsj.com; @S_Dockery)\par \par \uc2 16:12 ET - US stocks end marginally higher, though disappointing earnings from technology bellwethers hit the Nasdaq Composite. Alphabet falls 5.5% and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  loses 7.2% after posting weaker-than-expected results late Thursday. Outside of tech, sectors are generally stronger with higher oil prices once again lifting energy shares. DJIA gains 21 to 18003, the S&P 500 inches up 0.1 point at 2091, while the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  composite falls 39 to 4906. Economic news picks up pace next week, along with the continued flood of corporate earnings, with a Fed decision on Wednesday and the first reading on 1Q GDP Thursday. Bonds sold off this week, with the 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield posting its biggest gain since November. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:10 ET - Investors' lost their appetite for food stocks this week, one of the latest winning sectors they've turned on. The S&P 500's index of food-product manufacturers is down 1.6% this week, compared with a 0.4% gain in the broader market. The sector starts reporting Tuesday with Hershey (HSY), and as a whole is forecast to report an 8.9% decline in Q1 earnings, after a 10.2% decline forecast in February. Still, the food-products index is up 3.4% YTD, while the S&P 500 is up 2.3% YTD. (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 16:01 ET - Proofpoint's (PFPT) 1Q results topped estimates last light, and analysts says billings stole the show. Billings of $98M represent a 48% rise, and Wunderlich's Bob Breza tells the WSJ that the number is closer to 60% when you factor in the duration of billing cycles. CEO Gary Steele said in last night's analyst call that average contract duration came in "just below" the low end of its traditional 15-22 month range. Breza, modeling for a 14 month cycle, sees it as a good sign because "shorter duration billing cycles means the company will make more money." PFPT gains 5.7% to $56.74, up nearly 60% from a February low. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:00 ET - Pharmaceutical company \uc2 Novartis\uc2  (NOVN.EB) says it's under criminal investigation by Korean authorities, an acknowledgement that comes shortly after the company settled a foreign corruption investigation with the US. The disclosure in the company's securities filings comes after local reports of the company's Korean offices being searched by police. "In Q1 2016, the Seoul Western District Prosecutor initiated a criminal investigation into allegations that Novartis Korea utilized medical journals to provide inappropriate economic benefits to health-care professionals," the Swiss company says. "\uc2 Novartis\uc2  is cooperating with the investigation being conducted by the Seoul Western District Prosecutor's office," the company says in an email. (stephen.dockery@wsj.com; @S_Dockery)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2016 17:42 ET (21:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160422ec4m004mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
