\par\par \b \uc2 Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Declares a Shock-and-Awe Rate Hike -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 06:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 <FONT FACE="Arial" class="NormalMinus1">\par \par \uc2 DJ \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Declares a Shock-and-Awe Rate Hike -- WSJ Blog\par \par \uc2 By Paul Vigna\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declared war.\par \par \uc2 The bank raised interest rates to 12% from 7.75% at the conclusion of an emergency meeting convened late Tuesday night Ankara time to address the pummeled lira. This was far above the expected "emergency" rate hike; market consensus centered on a 2% rate hike. By going so much higher than expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  clearly was adopting a "shock and awe" maneuver to drive out a market assault. It seems to have worked, for now.\par \par \uc2 The unanswered questions are whether this will be a permanent solution, and what effect it will have on Turkey's economy.\par \par \uc2 "Recent domestic and external developments are having an adverse impact on risk perceptions, leading to a significant depreciation in the Turkish lira and a pronounced increase in the risk premium," the bank wrote in a brief statement. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will implement necessary measures at its disposal to contain the negative impact of these developments on inflation and macroeconomic stability."\par \par \uc2 This is the most extreme move the bank has made in the month or so that the lira's been under pressure. It tightened policy at its last meeting, then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week. None of that drove away the bears. Now comes this move, which had an immediate impact: The lira jumped 2.6% against the dollar in a matter of minutes.\par \par \uc2 "It's evident they've got the firepower to make something work, " said Scott Schuberg, CEO of Australian brokerage Rivkin. For now, he said, it seems to have done the trick, and drove out a lot of the speculative money. The thing to look out for, Mr. Schuberg cautioned, will be if the traders come back and "have another go at this one," and if the bank can defend the move.\par \par \uc2 "Let's see whether it's got legs," he said, noting even after the big move, the lira's only back to where it was about six weeks ago (Rivkin had been been long the dollar/lira, he said, but sold last night, ahead of the meeting).\par \par \uc2 The sharp slide in the lira was one of the triggers for the latest bout of selling of emerging-market assets last week. Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Turkey, Argentina, Russia and India sold off sharply, sparking two types of reactions: central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and politicians to blamed outsiders.\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, the administration of President \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  blamed big business and the media for the peso's slide. In Turkey, Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Ergodan blamed the media -- including The Wall Street Journal -- and the "interest rate lobby" for trying to capitalize on the country's problems.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrested the market selloff this week. \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move is about as dramatic as it gets, a clear sign from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will not allow the lira to just be relentlessly pounded. But raising rates so sharply, so suddenly, could be bad for the local economy, one of the reasons Mr. Ergodan was against the idea. Mr. Ergodan publicly said he was against interest-rate hikes, but acknowledged he couldn't prevent them.\par \par \uc2 "This seems like they are panicking," says National Alliance's Andrew Brenner. "It will not end well."\par \par \uc2 - Cynthia Lin contributed to this post.\par \par \uc2 More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 28, 2014 19:02 ET (00:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 29-01-14 0533GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020140129ea1t0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
