\par\par \b \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat Asia: Bulls Get Their Bounce -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vigna and Mia Lamar   \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 01:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Market Snap: At the New York close: S&P 500 up 0.6% at 1792.50. DJIA up 0.6% at 15928.56. Nasdaq Comp up 0.4% at 4097.96. Treasury yields fell; 10-year at 2.748%. Nymex crude oil up 1.8% at $97.41. Gold down 1% at $1,251.00/ounce.\par \par \uc2 How We Got Here: The Dow broke its losing streak as markets everywhere seemed to regain their balance as central banks stepped in to arrest the slides in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . India \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and then late Tuesday night, \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a big way.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  jacked up its overnight rate to 12% from 7.75%, an aggressive move meant to immediately shore up the lira. It worked. The lira jumped 2.6% against the dollar in the initial aftermath.\par \par \uc2 The irony of all this is that the biggest of the central banks, the Federal Reserve, started a two-day policy meeting. Its widely expected to announce on Wednesday that it's lopping another $10 billion off its QE3 stimulus program.\par \par \uc2 Don't get too excited about the respite for U.S. stocks. The Dow is still off 3.9% on the year, and the S&P 500 is off 3%.\par \par \uc2 Coming Up: No data on deck for Asia Wednesday; investors can expect a cautious session ahead of the FOMC decision later in the global trading day. Caution will also be running high ahead of a final reading Thursday on Chinese manufacturing activity for the month; a weaker-than-expected initial reading last week helped spark the recent global stock rout.\par \par \uc2 Turkey Raises Rates Sharply \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday unveiled a series of aggressive interest rate increases, far exceeding market expectations and sending the lira roaring back after an extraordinary policy meeting billed as a test case for under-pressure emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Stocks Rebound Stocks gained, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average breaking a five-session losing streak, as investors waited to see if the Federal Reserve will take another step in paring back its stimulus efforts.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine Turmoil Worries Kremlin Ukraine's president tried to placate demonstrators on Tuesday by accepting his prime minister's resignation and repealing new anti-dissent laws, but the heat remained on as Moscow signaled it could review a massive bailout deal.\par \par \uc2 Apple Shares Hit Three-Month Low Apple shares fell 8% Tuesday and hit their lowest point in three months after disappointing iPhone sales raised concerns about the company's growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 Apple, Samsung Confront New Rivals in China Apple and Samsung are the dominant smartphone brands in the U.S. and in many other parts of the world. But in China, the world's biggest smartphone market, there isn't a clear winner yet.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Surprises With Rate Hike India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a surprise increase in its key lending rate Tuesday, signaling its resolve to fight rising prices even as the South Asian economy faces its slowest growth in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Abe's History Lesson Haunts Davos Japan's Prime Minister, Shinzo Abe, managed to create a big stir with apparently offhand remarks to reporters that compared tensions between Japan and China to rivalries between Britain and Germany on the eve of World War I.\par \par \uc2 Thailand Government Holds to Plan for Voting Thailand's government Tuesday said it would go ahead with plans to hold national elections on Feb. 2, despite protests against the balloting and the opposition of the country's independent Election Commission.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street on Apple: 'Down But Not Out' Only Wall Street would find fault with a company that generated $13.1 billion in profit last quarter.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin's Future Gets a Hearing Bitcoin's future may have started taking shape in a conference room in lower Manhattan on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Rare Technical Event Warns of Resumed Slide For just the fourth time in the past 86 years, U.S. stocks are showing a pattern that this time around technicians say could mean a resumed slide for the market.\par \par \uc2 The Other Upcoming Rate Decision The South African Reserve Bank, which is also facing a swiftly falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is scheduled to meet Wednesday, having held its key interest rate on hold at a four-decade low of 5% since July 2012. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 28, 2014 19:09 ET (00:09 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140129ea1t0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
