\par\par \b \uc2 EM bond issuance gets off to a flier\b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 17:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * January record on course to be broken\par \par \uc2 * Dollar bond index up\par \par \uc2 * Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the doldrums\par \par \uc2 By Sudip Roy\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 23 (IFR) - Emerging markets bond issuance has got off to a roaring start with supply for the month on course to beat last January's new year spree.\par \par \uc2 About USD51.8bn had been issued in the international market up to January 21, according to Thomson Reuters, a 55% increase on the amount raised over the same period in 2013, and just USD10bn less than the volume issued for the full month of January. That record should be smashed in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 "Spreads are in, new issue premiums are negligible, it's the perfect storm," said one banker in London. "Who knows how long it's going to last."\par \par \uc2 Asia leads the way, accounting for roughly half of this year's volumes. While sovereigns, such as Indonesia, Sri Lanka and the Philippines have sold the big deals, the main theme, as last year, is the slew of property companies hitting the market.\par \par \uc2 The supply has not surprised bankers, who say that many Chinese developers have maturities coming up this year, and with local rates higher, it makes sense for them to tap the offshore bond market.\par \par \uc2 However, some stresses are beginning to show in the property sector. \uc2 Powerlong Real Estate Holdings\uc2  earlier this week became the first emerging markets issuer to postpone a new issue this year, delaying an offshore renminbi transaction.\par \par \uc2 The developer did not give a reason, though rival bankers attributed the postponement mostly to some investors asking for higher yields.\par \par \uc2 There then followed a generous new dollar bond from Wanda Properties that dragged the rest of the sector down. "The property sector is fragile and is vulnerable, and Wanda's deal has just repriced the sector," said a high-yield trader.\par \par \uc2 Still, those concerns aside, most emerging markets new issues have gone smoothly, with Turkey's USD2.5bn 10-year transaction on Wednesday in particular seen as an encouraging sign of its market access given the political problems in the country.\par \par \uc2 POSITIVE PERFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 Another encouraging sign, and in contrast to the beginning of last year, is that secondary performance is holding steady, with emerging markets dollar-denominated bonds up nearly 1%, according to \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's EMBI index.\par \par \uc2 That positive number will provide a fillip to investors after a disastrous 2013, when many were nursing losses of 6% or more. Indeed, all emerging markets sub-asset classes performed terribly last year, but since the beginning of this year hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign debt has become more resilient.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds remain in the doldrums, with returns on indices for both in negative territory, driven by dollar strength and exacerbated by worries about slowing economies and growing political risks in several countries.\par \par \uc2 In Turkey, for example, the lira has fallen to a record low even after the successful bond sale as a corruption probe has ramped up political tensions. With the lira extending losses against the US dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made its first direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for two years, selling around USD2bn-USD2.5bn of its reserves - more or less the amount it raised through the bond sale.\par \par \uc2 The hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge, however, is being driven more by technical factors. With the Fed's tapering programme under way, investors feel more comfortable putting their cash to work, which still remains plentiful despite bond outflows from emerging market funds over past year.\par \par \uc2 Although there were USD11.1bn of redemptions from hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds last year, according to EPFR Global, this is more than offset by the amount of incoming money from amortizations and coupon payments on outstanding bonds.\par \par \uc2 "EM hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond investors have continued to be net receivers of capital, which in turn explains the large demand for new issuance," said Regis Chatellier, emerging markets senior credit strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors, though, remain cautious about the medium-term prospects of both hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt.\par \par \uc2 "After the underperformance of the asset class in the second half of last year, both emerging market US dollar-denominated sovereign and corporate bonds are beginning to look attractive relative to developed market corporates," said Benjamin Brodsky, head of fixed income asset allocation at BlackRock.\par \par \uc2 "But emerging markets, especially local bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will remain volatile and ultimately differentiation will drive return opportunities."   (Reporting by Sudip Roy; Editing by Julian Baker)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-1-26T16:17:34.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140123ea1n00p8v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
